.TH KARICTL 8 "2026-11-24" "KariDNS" "KariDNS Manual"
.\" Generated from docs/karictl.md by man/md2man.pl (make man): edit the Markdown manual, not this page.
.SH NAME
karictl \- Remote Control and Management Utility for KariDNS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS 2
.nf
karictl [\-c config_path | \-f config_path] [\-s socket_path] [\-t seconds] <command> [arguments...]
karictl \-v | \-\-version
karictl \-h | \-\-help
karictl tsig\-keygen [keyname]
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBkarictl\fR is the management utility for the \fBkaridns(8)\fR authoritative
DNS server daemon. It communicates with the daemon over a local UNIX domain
socket (default \fB/var/run/karidns/control.sock\fR) and authenticates each
connection with an HMAC challenge-response handshake: the server sends a
64-character challenge, and \fBkarictl\fR answers with the HMAC of the challenge
keyed with the shared secret. The HMAC algorithm is \fBcontrol\-channel {
algorithm }\fR on the server and \fBalgorithm\fR in \fBkarictl.conf\fR (both
default to \fBhmac\-sha256\fR).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 8
\fB\-c config_path\fR, \fB\-f config_path\fR
Path to the \fBkarictl\fR configuration file (default:
\fB/usr/local/etc/karidns/karictl.conf\fR; if that file does not exist and
\fB/usr/local/etc/karictl.conf\fR does, the latter is used).
.TP 8
\fB\-s socket_path\fR
UNIX domain socket to connect to. Takes precedence over the \fBsocket\fR setting
of the configuration file (default: \fB/var/run/karidns/control.sock\fR).
.TP 8
\fB\-t seconds\fR
How long to wait for each part of the server's reply: the challenge, and then
each read of the answer (1\[u2013]86400, default 60, as \fBrndc \-t\fR). When
the server sends nothing for that long, \fBkarictl\fR prints \fBno reply from
the server within N s\fR and fails. A full \fBreload\fR of the zone files
answers when the reload is done, so a server with very many zone files may need
a larger value; database zones answer at once (\fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows the
progress).
.TP 8
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Display version information and exit.
.TP 8
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print the command list with the \fBreload <zone> [view] [force]\fR syntax and
examples, and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
Commands that take a zone name accept an optional view name as the next
argument. The view name is required when the zone exists in more than one view;
otherwise the command fails with \fBERROR zone exists in multiple views; specify
view\fR.
.TP 8
\fBstatus\fR
Query and display runtime statistics from the running \fBkaridns\fR daemon:
.RS 8
.IP \(bu 2
Version, host name, OS, architecture and kernel release and CPU count of the
running server (taken when it started), and its worker thread count
.IP \(bu 2
Server boot time, time of the last successful (re)configuration (both to the
second) and the configuration file path
.PP
\fBkarictl\fR and \fBkaridns\fR must be the same version: the reply is a binary
structure, and a reply of another size is shown as is and exits with status 3.
.IP \(bu 2
Number of zones, \fBdebug level: N\fR (the debug level in force: \fBkaridns
\-d\fR, \fBtrace\fR, \fBnotrace\fR; where \fBrndc status\fR prints it) and
number of running AXFR/IXFR transfers
.IP \(bu 2
Whether query and response logging are enabled
.IP \(bu 2
Current and high-water TCP connection counts
.IP \(bu 2
Size of the zone versions and IXFR deltas (D1a): the bytes of the published
versions of the zones (the version, its image and its glue block) and of the
IXFR deltas the zones keep (the records of the difference and the data in them
as it is used, with the overhead of each block: a difference of one SOA counts
about 1.8 KB; 0.7), for the zones that come from the database and for the
others, as \fBresident\fR (what the zones serve now) and \fBretired\fR (versions
that a newer one replaced and that are not freed yet, because a reader or a
transfer may still hold them). Since 0.7 the bytes of the zone entry itself (the
entry, the lock it makes, a database zone's settings block, about 0.85 KB) are
in it, counted with the zone's first version, and the images and glue blocks are
counted at the size the allocator really holds. The directory, the response
cache and the park tables are not in it. An IXFR delta that left the history
stays counted as resident until the last transfer that reads it has released
it. A database zone that \fBmemory\-limit\fR takes out of memory gives up its
IXFR deltas at once (they are not served while the zone is out of memory, and
the zone comes back without them), so evicting and fetching a zone again does
not add to the resident bytes. Read only: nothing limits them yet
.IP \(bu 2
The bytes the zones keep for \fBrollback\fR (the previous versions, see
\fBrollback\fR): \fBzone versions kept for rollback: database zones N bytes,
other zones N bytes\fR. They are not served; they are freed when the
\fBrollback\-window\fR is over, or earlier when the database \fBmemory\-limit\fR
needs the room
.IP \(bu 2
What waits to be freed (0.7): \fBretired zone snapshots waiting: N (oldest N
ms)\fR (zone-db snapshots that a reload or a change replaced and that a reader
still holds: a dynamic update, a control command, a zone transfer that waits for
a database zone to be read (\fBtransfer\-fetch\-timeout\fR), or a thread inside
a read section; they are freed in order, so one held snapshot keeps the later
ones too. A transfer that is being sent holds none, 0.7 R11), \fBretired zone
versions waiting: N\fR (zone versions and objects on the retire stack, not freed
yet: a reader that stays in its read section keeps every version replaced after
it entered (a reload, an UPDATE, a database batch never wait for it: the count
rises and falls when it leaves), a reader holds them, or the version is the one
a running outbound transfer sends: it is freed when that transfer ends) and
\fBreclaim warnings (snapshot held over 60 s): N\fR. Nothing limits these and no
reader is cut off; when the oldest snapshot has waited more than 60 s, karidns
writes a warning in the category \fBgeneral\fR (\fB[GC] the oldest retired zone
snapshot has waited N s ...: held by ...\fR, naming the kind of reader slot or
the retained references), at most once a minute while it waits
.IP \(bu 2
Expired secondaries (0.7): \fBsecondaries expired: N (S serving stale, F
SERVFAIL)\fR, the secondary zones past their expire deadline (the control
thread's last walk of the secondaries), those answered from stale data
(\fBserve\-stale\fR, EDE 3) and those answered SERVFAIL (EDE 24; karidns(8)
"Secondary zones: the copies in the zone store"); \fBstats\fR has them as the
gauge \fBkaridns_secondaries_expired{answer="stale"|"servfail"}\fR and, in JSON,
\fBsecondaries_expired\fR \fB{total, stale, servfail}\fR in \fBserver\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Frontend process health status
.IP \(bu 2
Security counters: RRL dropped/slipped responses, Extended DNS Error counters
(18 Prohibited, 20 Not Authoritative, 21 Not Supported, other) and truncated
dnstap messages
.IP \(bu 2
IPC drops (0.7): \fBIPC dropped (router/worker queues, see karictl ipcstats):
N\fR, the queries and replies that were lost between the Frontend routers and
the Backend workers because a queue was full (or for another reason) and that no
worker answered; \fBipcstats\fR has each place. 0 is what a server should
show. The line is left out by a server of a version without \fBipcstats\fR
.IP \(bu 2
dnstap (0.7): \fBdnstap dropped (queue full): N\fR (messages lost because a ring
was full: the collector reads too slowly, or \fBqueue\-size\fR is too small),
\fBdnstap dropped (not connected): N\fR (messages lost while no collector was
connected), \fBdnstap connected: yes|no|no (not configured)\fR and \fBdnstap
reconnects: N\fR (connections made by the background retry); see karidns(8)
\fBdnstap\fR "Loss". Without a \fBdnstap\fR block the counters are 0, as
\fBdnstap truncated\fR is
.RE
.TP 8
\fBreload [\-force | zone [view] [force]]\fR
Without arguments, re-read the configuration file and \fBall\fR zone files (see
\fBreconfig\fR for the replies; a full reload also drops the changes of dynamic
updates), then, when the configuration has a \fBdatabase\fR block, ask the
database loader to compare its databases with the zones served: the loader sends
the zones that are new, whose serial differs, that the change log
(\fBkaridns_changes\fR, see \fBdb/karidns_ext.*.sql\fR) lists as edited, or
whose last version was refused, and a \fBZONE_DELETE\fR for the zones that left
the database. The reply does not wait for the loader: \fBOK reloaded; DB reload
requested (request N)\fR; \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows the batch when it is
done. With a zone name, reload only that zone: a primary zone re-reads its zone
file (errors such as a missing file, a parse error, a missing SOA or a running
AXFR are reported), and a secondary zone starts a new transfer from its
primary. For a \fBprogram zone\fR (\fBtype program\fR, 0.6.2) the operator who
names the zone asks for its data to be read again, and the plugin is dealt with
by the first of these that applies: (1) the zone's program settings
(\fBprogram\fR, \fBprogram\-args\fR, \fBprogram\-user\fR,
\fBprogram\-timeout\fR, \fBprogram\-max\-failures\fR,
\fBdisable\-auto\-tc\-flag\fR, \fBprogram\-reload\-line\fR) in the active
configuration are not those of its running plugin: a new plugin is started, and
when it is up the old one is stopped, as for a reload; (2) the settings are the
same and the zone has \fBprogram\-reload\-line yes;\fR: the plugin gets the
\fBRELOAD\fR line and is not restarted; (3) the settings are the same and the
zone has no \fBprogram\-reload\-line\fR (or \fBno\fR): a plugin without that
line can read its data again only by starting again, so a new plugin with the
same settings is started and replaces the old one in the same way (queries are
answered throughout). This differs from a full \fBreload\fR (and \fBreconfig\fR,
\fBSIGHUP\fR), which leaves a plugin whose settings did not change alone, with
the \fBRELOAD\fR line where configured. The reload of a zone does not read
\fBkaridns.conf\fR again (run \fBreconfig\fR or a full \fBreload\fR first to
apply an edit; those already replace the plugins of changed zones, so case 1
arises when that replacement was refused or has not been answered yet, and the
reload asks for it again at once). \fBforce\fR has no effect. The replies: \fBOK
reloaded (program zone: the program settings changed; a new plugin was asked
for, the old one answers until it is up)\fR (1), \fBOK reloaded (program zone:
RELOAD line sent to the plugin)\fR (2), \fBOK reloaded (program zone: no
program\-reload\-line; a new plugin with the same settings was asked for, the
old one answers until it is up)\fR (3), \fBOK reloaded (program zone: a plugin
was asked for; the zone answers when it is up)\fR (a plugin that never came up,
or a refused replacement, is asked for again), \fBOK reloaded (program zone: a
new plugin with these settings is already on its way)\fR, and \fBERROR no plugin
runs for this zone: allow\-program\-zones is not yes, or the zone has no
program\fR. \fBreload \-force\fR (0.7, the zone store) is the full reload for
which the loader lists every zone of its databases again (a manifest pass)
instead of reading the change log from its cursor. With the zone store the
Backend deletes the zones of a view that the pass does not list, unless that
would delete more than 20 percent of the view's zones and more than 1,000: then
nothing is deleted, \fBERROR\fR is logged, \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows
\fBMASS\-DELETE GUARD HELD\fR and \fBkarictl store\fR has
\fBstore.recon.guard.*\fR. \fBThe operator's release of that guard is \fBreload
\-force\fB\fR: the pass it asks for is not held back, the zones the database no
longer lists are deleted and the guard is let go. It needs the permission
\fBreload\fR of a restricted control key, like a full \fBreload\fR. A zone that
is not in \fBkaridns.conf\fR is looked up in the database. For a \fBdatabase
zone\fR the loader reads that zone only (also a zone just added to the database,
which the server does not know yet). When several views take database zones and
no view is named, the loader is asked about every one of them (a mapping in
\fBkaridns_domain_views\fR may have moved the zone) and the reply is one line,
\fB<z>: view <v1>: <result>; view <v2>: <result>\fR, \fBOK\fR when at least one
view has the zone and \fBERROR\fR when it is rejected or failed there or when no
view has it (\fBzone not found (it is not in the database of any view that takes
database zones)\fR); a named view gives the single-view replies below, converts
it, and sends it when its content differs from the version the server serves,
whatever its serial; \fBforce\fR sends it even when the content is the same
(D1a: not a zone that is out of memory and unchanged; there is nothing in memory
to replace, the next query reads it from the database, and the reply is
\fBunchanged, serial <n> (not in memory)\fR). With the zone store (0.7) a reload
of one zone without \fBforce\fR is decided by the version stamp of the database
(\fBkaridns_zone_state\fR; the serial for a source without stamps; a zone
without a stamp is always read): a zone that did not change is \fBunchanged,
serial <n> (not in memory)\fR and is not read, one that changed has its body
read, and the answer waits for it; \fBforce\fR reads the body whatever the stamp
says, also for a zone that is not in memory (nearly every zone of a big server
is stored and not in memory). \fBkarictl\fR waits for the loader's answer, but
the server does not: the connection is parked and answered when the result
arrives, or after \fBdb\-reload\-timeout\fR seconds (\fBERROR timeout: ...\fR;
the reload may still be applied). At most 32 clients wait at once; the next ones
get \fBERROR too many reloads are waiting for the database loader; try again
later\fR. Replies (all \fBOK\fR unless marked):
.RS 8
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOK zone <z> (view <v>): applied, serial <n>\fR: the new version is served.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOK zone <z> (view <v>): unchanged, serial <n>\fR: the loader found the
content equal to the served version. With memory-limit, \fB (not in memory)\fR
follows for a zone whose data is out of memory; it is not read, also with
\fBforce\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOK zone <z> (view <v>): loaded, serial <n>, but hidden by the zone in
karidns.conf\fR (or \fB... by a catalog member of the same name\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOK zone <z> (view <v>): deleted (it is no longer in the database)\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBERROR zone <z> (view <v>): rejected: <reason>; the last good version, if any,
keeps serving\fR: the server refused the zone.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBERROR zone <z> (view <v>): failed: <reason>\fR: the loader could not read or
convert the zone (a bad row, a missing SOA, an unreadable database); the last
good version keeps serving.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBERROR zone not found (...)\fR: the database does not have the zone (or the
view does not exist).
.IP \(bu 2
several views, none named: \fBOK zone <z>: view a: not in the database; view b:
applied, serial <n>\fR: the result of each view that takes database zones, in
configuration order (\fBnot in the database\fR, \fBapplied, serial <n>\fR,
\fBunchanged, serial <n>\fR, \fBdeleted (...)\fR, \fBrejected: ...\fR,
\fBfailed: ...\fR).
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karictl reload                       # configuration, zone files, and a comparison of the databases
karictl reload example.test          # one zone, from its file or from the database
karictl reload example.test force    # send the database zone even if it is unchanged
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP 8
\fBreconfig\fR
Re-read the configuration file and apply it (added or removed zones, ACLs, rate
limits, ...). Zone files whose modification time and size have not changed are
not re-read. This is the same as sending \fBSIGHUP\fR to the server. When the
\fBzone { }\fR blocks of every view are the ones of the running configuration,
the zones served from the database are taken over as they are (a reload at
1,000,000 database zones takes about 30 ms instead of walking every zone), and
the loader is told about the new configuration afterwards. \fBreload\fR and
\fBreconfig\fR answer when the configuration and the zones are applied, not when
the memory they replaced is freed (0.7): a thread that stays in a read section
of the server only keeps that memory longer (\fBretired zone versions waiting\fR
in \fBstatus\fR). \fBreload\fR (without arguments) and \fBreconfig\fR answer:
.RS 8
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOK\fR (\fBOK reloaded\fR for \fBreload\fR): the configuration was applied and
every zone loaded. When a setting that needs a restart changed, \fB(restart
needed for: port, ...)\fR follows.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBERROR configuration applied, but N zone(s) failed to load: <zones>\fR: the
new configuration is in use, and the listed zones (also logged to syslog) answer
SERVFAIL until their files can be loaded. A zone transfer that is being sent
does not make a zone fail (0.7: before, a reload waited 5 s for it and listed
the zone here); it goes on with the version it started with.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBERROR configuration not applied: <reason>\fR: the file could not be read, has
an error (see syslog or \fBkaricheck conf\fR), or a log file could not be
opened; the running configuration is kept.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBERROR reload postponed: ...\fR: a thread of the server was still reading the
configuration before the last one. The configuration is kept in two buffers and
the new one is read into the one that thread may still hold, so the reload is
not done now. \fBreload\fR and \fBreconfig\fR first wait for such readers as
long as they last in normal operation: the longest \fBprogram\-timeout\fR or
\fBforward\-timeout\fR of the configuration plus 0.5 s, at least 1 s and at most
5 s (a worker that waits for a program zone's plugin or a forward zone's
forwarders reads the configuration meanwhile). Only a reader that stays longer
gives this reply, within that time. The server then applies the reload by
itself, with what the command was to do (\fBreload\fR: every zone file and the
request to the database loader), within a second of the reader leaving; the log
says when (\fBThe postponed reload was applied\fR). karictl exits with status 3
for this reply, as for every \fBERROR\fR. A zone transfer that waits for a slow
client is not such a reader (0.7).
.PP
Every \fBERROR\fR reply makes \fBkarictl\fR exit with status 3.
.RE
.TP 8
\fBstop\fR
Stop the \fBkaridns\fR daemon.
.TP 8
\fBnotify <zone> [view]\fR
Send DNS NOTIFY messages for \fB<zone>\fR now: to the \fBalso\-notify\fR servers
and to the addresses of the zone's apex NS hosts, except the host named in the
SOA MNAME field (RFC 1996 \[u00A7]3.2). Zones served from a database are found
too; one that is hidden by a configured zone or a catalog member of the same
name answers \fBERROR zone is not served from the database ...\fR. A database
zone of the zone store (0.7) that is not in memory is attached first (the answer
is \fBOK (the zone is not in memory: it is attached first)\fR), and its NOTIFY
is sent when it is there; if a name server of the zone is in another stored zone
that is not in memory, that zone is asked for too and the NOTIFY waits for it
for a second at the most.
.TP 8
\fBretransfer <zone> [view]\fR
For a secondary zone (including a catalog member zone), discard the current
serial and start a new transfer (AXFR) from the primary immediately. For any
other zone the server answers \fBERROR zone is not a secondary\fR and karictl
exits with status 3; the zone is not changed. A \fBSLAVE\fR or \fBCONSUMER\fR
zone from the database (milestone B7b) is a secondary and is transferred again
like any other; a \fBNATIVE\fR / \fBMASTER\fR zone from the database is a
primary zone: the answer is \fBERROR zone is a primary zone from the database;
read it again with 'karictl reload <zone>'\fR.
.TP 8
\fBrollback <zone> [view] [local]\fR
Put the zone back to the version it had before its last reload. The server keeps
the version that a \fBzone-file reload\fR (\fBreload\fR, \fBreload <zone>\fR,
\fBreconfig\fR that re-reads the file) or an \fBinstall from the database\fR
replaces, for \fBrollback\-window\fR (default 1 hour; \fB0\fR keeps nothing;
\fBoptions\fR, \fBview\fR and \fBzone\fR, see karidns(8)). A dynamic update, a
zone transfer and a rollback do not replace it: after a reload and three
updates, \fBrollback\fR goes back to the version before the reload and the three
updates are gone with it. A reload that brings the very same content does not
replace it either, so a second \fBreload\fR of the file you just fixed does not
push the good version out. The old content is published again with \fBSOA serial
= the current serial + 1\fR (RFC 1982; a serial of 4294967295 gives 1), an IXFR
difference is recorded and NOTIFY is sent, as for a reload, so the secondaries
follow and never see a serial that goes back. Reply: \fBOK rolled back: serial
<new> (was <current>)\fR. \fBzonestatus\fR shows \fB rolled back (serial <new>,
from <current>)\fR until the next publication (a reload, an update, a database
install), and, while a previous version is kept, \fB previous version kept
(serial <n>, <bytes> bytes, <s> s left)\fR. With \fBlocal\fR the old version is
published \fBunchanged, with its old serial, on this server only\fR: no NOTIFY
is sent and the IXFR history of the zone is dropped, so a secondary that has the
newer serial does not follow (it never accepts a lower serial) and a secondary
that is behind transfers the whole zone. Use it for a signed zone, or to look at
the old data. The view name is written first when there are both: \fBrollback
example.test internal local\fR (a view that is itself named \fBlocal\fR is
written twice: \fBrollback example.test local local\fR). Replies that are
\fBERROR\fR (karictl exits with 3, nothing is changed):
.RS 8
.IP \(bu 2
\fBno previous version: ...\fR: the zone was not reloaded within
\fBrollback\-window\fR since it was loaded, the window ran out, or it was rolled
back already (\fBonce per reload\fR; there is no redo; reload the file again to
get a version to go back to).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrollback\-window is 0 for this zone: no previous version is kept\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrollback is for primary zones; a secondary gets its data from the primary\fR:
a secondary, a catalog member, a \fBforward\fR zone. A catalog zone (\fBa
catalog zone cannot be rolled back\fR) and a \fBprogram\fR zone are refused too.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBzone is signed; roll back in the DB and re\-sign\fR: the version to go back
to holds RRSIG or DNSKEY records. KariDNS cannot sign, and a new serial would
leave the SOA with a signature that does not match. Roll back where the zone is
signed, or use \fBlocal\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBthe zone was taken out of memory; nothing to roll back\fR (a database zone
whose data left memory, or one hidden by a zone of the same name). What a later
reload does:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBZone file.\fR \fBreload <zone>\fR and a full \fBreload\fR keep the
rolled-back version while the file is the one that was read (same modification
time and size), and say so (\fBOK kept the rolled\-back version: ...\fR). Fix
the file and reload, or \fBreload <zone> force\fR to read it again (which brings
the data back that you rolled back). A change only in a file that the zone
\fB$INCLUDE\fRs is not noticed by that check: use \fBforce\fR. \fBreconfig\fR
already skips files that did not change. A restart reads the file again.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDatabase zone in the zone store (0.7).\fR A zone that is in memory is found
like any other. One that is not in memory has no previous version to go back to
(the store holds the current image only; the version before the last one is kept
with the zone in memory): the answer is \fBERROR the zone is in the zone store
and not in memory: it has no previous version to roll back to ...\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDatabase zone.\fR The server compares the loader's (source id, serial,
digest) with what it recorded when it installed the zone, and a rollback does
not change that record, so the rolled-back version stays until the data in the
database change; the next change is installed normally. Meanwhile the zone is
pinned in memory like one that a dynamic update changed (\fBdbstatus <zone>\fR
says \fBrolled back: serial N, from M; pinned in memory ...\fR), because the
database cannot give the old version back. With several servers on one database,
a rollback on one makes them differ until the database changes; a rollback in
the database (\fBkaridns_*\fR data) is the durable fix.
.IP \(bu 2
The command needs the permission \fBrollback\fR of a restricted control key
(\fBallow\-commands { rollback; }\fR), and is logged with the key name like the
other commands.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karictl reload example.test          # the new file has a mistake
karictl rollback example.test        # serial N+1, the old data, the secondaries follow
karictl rollback example.test local  # the old version as it was, this server only
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP 8
\fBzonestatus <zone> [view]\fR
Display the SOA serial and refresh interval of \fB<zone>\fR (\fBOK serial=<n>
refresh=<n> retry=<n> expire=<n>\fR; for a secondary the values the zone uses,
held to \fBmin\-refresh\-time\fR ... \fBmax\-retry\-time\fR). For a zone from
the database the line goes on with \fBsource=database view=<v> id=<domains.id>
result=<applied|rejected|shadowed (hidden by ...)>\fR and the last error, if
any; a zone of the zone store (0.7) that is not in memory shows the record of
the store (its serial and id) and \fB(in the zone store, not in memory)\fR, or
\fB(in the zone store without a body yet: ...)\fR for a zone the store knows and
has not read from the database yet. A secondary that applies settings received
from its primary (Extended AXFR version 3, see karidns(8)) adds
\fBsettings=primary\fR (and \fBnote: ...\fR when part of them is not in force,
e.g. a TSIG key the received \fBallow\-transfer\fR names is not defined here). A
secondary (0.7: of karidns.conf, a catalog member, or a database secondary) adds
lines: \fBStore: copy serial <n>, <bytes> bytes, saved <UTC>\fR (its copy in the
zone store; when the primary sent settings with the zone, followed by \fB, the
settings of the primary kept\fR or \fB, the settings of the primary not kept (a
restart asks for the whole zone)\fR; or \fBStore: no copy\fR, \fBStore: off\fR
without a store, \fBStore: restore pending\fR while the start has not looked at
the copy yet, \fBStore: not kept (...)\fR for a zone whose copy cannot be
named), \fBLast\-refresh: <UTC>  Last\-attempt: <UTC> [(failed)]  Expires:
<UTC>\fR, and when the zone is expired \fBExpired: since <UTC> (<duration>),
serving stale data (serve\-stale)\fR or \fBExpired: since <UTC> (<duration>),
answering SERVFAIL (serve\-stale no | secondary\-stale\-max <d> reached)\fR
(karidns(8) "Secondary zones: the copies in the zone store"). \fBkarictl
store\fR lists the copies as the \fBxfr.*\fR and \fBsecondary.*\fR keys.
.TP 8
\fBobservatory [zone [view]]\fR
Display per-zone statistics for all zones, or only for the given zone (and
view): role (primary/secondary), SOA serial, query counts (total and TCP),
response codes (NOERROR, NXDOMAIN, NODATA, SERVFAIL, REFUSED), EDNS/DO/ECS query
counts, RRL dropped/slipped counts, successful inbound AXFR/IXFR transfers
(secondary, with \fBFailed\-refreshes\fR: the refreshes that failed at every
primary asked) or NOTIFY messages sent (primary) with the time of the last one,
and response cache hits and misses (the cache is shared by all zones, so it has
no per-zone size). A zone that other views share with \fBzone { in\-view }\fR
(karidns(8) "Zones shared between views") is listed once, in the view that owns
it, as \fBZone: <name> (view: <owner>, shared to views: <a>, <b>)\fR;
\fBstatus\fR counts it once, and \fBnotify\fR, \fBzonestatus\fR and \fBreload\fR
name it with or without a view (a referencing view's name reaches the owner's
zone).
.TP 8
\fBstats [\-f prometheus|json] [zone [view]] [\-\-zones PATTERN] [\-\-limit N] [\-\-after CURSOR]\fR
Print the counters of the server, its views and zones, for monitoring. The
default format is the Prometheus text exposition format (metric names
\fBkaridns_*\fR, labels \fBzone\fR, \fBview\fR, \fBrcode\fR, \fBproto\fR, ...),
which the node_exporter textfile collector reads; \fB\-f json\fR (or
\fB\-\-format json\fR) prints one JSON object. (\fB\-f\fR after \fBstats\fR is
the format; the \fB\-f\fR before the command is still the configuration
file.) The exit status is 0 only when the whole output was written, so
\fBkarictl stats > file.$$ && mv file.$$ file\fR never leaves a half-written
file. The output has a \fBserver\fR part (version, boot time, zone count,
workers, TCP clients, zone memory, query, response, EDNS, NOTIFY, transfer,
response-cache, rate-limit and Extended DNS Error counters, failed transfers,
failed reloads, raised alerts, the reclaim queue (the gauges
\fBkaridns_reclaim_snapshots_waiting\fR,
\fBkaridns_reclaim_oldest_snapshot_age_seconds\fR,
\fBkaridns_reclaim_zone_versions_waiting\fR and the counter
\fBkaridns_reclaim_warnings_total\fR; in JSON \fBreclaim\fR
\fB{snapshots_waiting, oldest_snapshot_age_ms, zone_versions_waiting,
warnings}\fR in \fBserver\fR) and dnstap (\fBkaridns_dnstap_truncated_total\fR,
\fBkaridns_dnstap_dropped_total{reason="queue_full"|"not_connected"}\fR, the
gauge \fBkaridns_dnstap_connected\fR (1 or 0; 0 also without a \fBdnstap\fR
block) and \fBkaridns_dnstap_reconnects_total\fR; in JSON
\fBdnstap_truncated\fR, \fBdnstap_dropped\fR \fB{queue_full, not_connected}\fR,
\fBdnstap_connected\fR (true / false) and \fBdnstap_reconnects\fR)), then
\fBviews\fR (zones, queries and responses summed per view; only when every zone
is listed), then one set of series \fBper zone\fR (queries by transport,
responses by RCODE, EDNS / DO / ECS queries, rate-limit drops and slips, NOTIFY,
transfers in and their failures, response cache hits and misses, SOA serial,
role, time of the last transfer). The counters are cumulative since the server
started (Prometheus \fBcounter\fRs); a zone that a reload replaces starts again
at 0, which \fBrate()\fR handles as a counter reset. The queries and replies
that were lost between the routers and the workers (0.7, \fBipcstats\fR below)
are in the server part too:
\fBkaridns_ipc_events_total{event="router.worker.dropped_full"}\fR and the other
events of \fBipcstats\fR (the name without the \fBipc.\fR prefix;
\fBrouter.worker.spilled\fR is not a loss), in JSON \fBipc\fR
\fB{"router.worker.spilled":N, ...}\fR. They are left out when the server does
not know \fBipcstats\fR. Queries that no zone answered (REFUSED for a name
outside every zone, FORMERR, NOTIMP, ...) belong to no zone: they are in
\fBkaridns_unzoned_responses_total{rcode}\fR, and
\fBkaridns_responses_total{rcode}\fR is the zones' responses plus
those. \fBkaridns_queries_total{proto}\fR counts the queries answered for a
zone. \fBstats <zone> [view]\fR prints one zone (the same as \fBobservatory
<zone>\fR, without the other zones). Without a zone name every zone is listed,
fetched in pages of 2000 (the server never builds one reply with all zones) in
the order of its zone tables (the order of the names' hashes, not alphabetical;
a zone added or removed while the listing runs may or may not be
seen). \fB\-\-zones PATTERN\fR lists only the zones whose name matches the glob
(\fB*\fR, \fB?\fR, \fB[a\-z]\fR, at most four \fB*\fR; case-insensitive;
\fBexample.*\fR matches \fBexample.test.\fR), \fB\-\-limit N\fR stops after N
zones and, if there are more, ends the output with \fB# more zones: karictl
stats \-\-after <cursor>\fR (JSON: \fB"more":true,"after":"<cursor>"\fR), and
\fB\-\-after CURSOR\fR continues there. A cursor is a position in the server's
zone tables, so a page costs the server the page, not the number of zones (at
most 65536 table slots are looked at for one page; a glob that matches little
gives short pages that karictl asks for one after the other). A cursor from
before a reload that rebuilt the tables is refused (\fBcursor is stale\fR, exit
3); a text listing that meets this while it runs starts again (up to three
times), a JSON listing, which is written as it comes, stops with the error. The
totals of the zones' counters in the server part (queries by transport, NODATA,
EDNS, NOTIFY, transfers) are summed with one walk of all zones per command (see
the release notes for the time at 1,000,000 zones). Values with white space are
refused (write a space in a name as \fB\e032\fR). The text format is valid
exposition format whatever the number of zones: karictl collects the zones first
(184 bytes and the zone name, about 25 bytes, per zone: about 210 MB for
1,000,000 zones; use \fB\-\-zones\fR and \fB\-\-limit\fR to bound it) and writes
every metric family once, in one piece, with \fB# HELP\fR and \fB# TYPE\fR
once. \fB\-f json\fR is written as the pages come and keeps nothing. The command
needs the read permission of a restricted control key, like \fBobservatory\fR.
.RS 8
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karictl stats > /var/tmp/node_exporter/karidns.prom.$$ && mv /var/tmp/node_exporter/karidns.prom.$$ /var/tmp/node_exporter/karidns.prom
karictl stats \-f json example.test
karictl stats \-\-zones '*.example.test' \-\-limit 100
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP 8
\fBipcstats\fR
The queries and replies that were lost, or moved, between the Frontend routers
and the Backend workers (0.7), as lines \fBipc.<name>=<number>\fR: counters
since the server started, summed over the routers and the workers. The reply is
text (a \fBkarictl\fR of another version reads what is there: a name it does not
know is printed or left out, never an error). Needs the read permission of a
restricted control key.
.RS 8
.TP 8
\fBipc.router.worker.spilled\fR
queries that the worker chosen for a batch could not take (its queue was full)
and another worker took: not a loss. A router sends the datagrams it read (up to
16 at a time) to one worker, in turn; a worker that is busy for a moment no
longer costs the batch, the rest goes to the next worker, at most once round all
of them
.TP 8
\fBipc.router.worker.dropped_full\fR
queries dropped because every worker's queue was full: each worker was tried
.TP 8
\fBipc.router.worker.dropped_error\fR
queries dropped because the send failed for another reason (\fBEMSGSIZE\fR, ...)
.TP 8
\fBipc.router.rx.truncated\fR, \fBipc.router.rx.short\fR
datagrams that the router dropped: larger than its receive buffer
(\fBMSG_TRUNC\fR), or shorter than a DNS header (12 octets)
.TP 8
\fBipc.router.reply.short\fR, \fB.bad_length\fR, \fB.bad_socket\fR
replies of a worker that the router dropped as malformed: shorter than the IPC
header, a length that does not fit the message, a socket number that does not
exist. Since 0.7 B3 phase 3 a worker packs the replies of one batch into few
datagrams (frames, see \fBudp\-receive\fR in \fBkaridns(8)\fR), and these count
the frames: a reply of no octet is a bad length too
.TP 8
\fBipc.router.client.dropped_full\fR, \fB.dropped_error\fR
replies that the router could not send to the client: the UDP send buffer was
full, or another error. A full queue (\fBENOBUFS\fR: the network interface's
transmit queue; \fBEAGAIN\fR) is waited for first: the router sends the rest of
the batch again, in order, for at most 300 microseconds, and only what is still
refused then is dropped and counted here; a socket that used up that time is
tried once per batch, without waiting, until a batch goes out whole
.TP 8
\fBipc.router.client.retried\fR
replies that the client socket refused for a moment and took when the router
sent them again: not a loss, not in \fBipc.dropped.total\fR
.TP 8
\fBipc.worker.rx.short\fR, \fBipc.worker.rx.bad_length\fR
messages of a router that a worker dropped as malformed
.TP 8
\fBipc.worker.reply.dropped_full\fR, \fB.dropped_error\fR
replies that a worker had answered and the queue to the router did not take:
full, or another error (counted in replies, also when several went in one
datagram). A full queue is waited for first (0.7): the worker sends the refused
datagram again, in order, at once and then after pauses of 1 millisecond that
leave the CPU to the router, for at most 50 milliseconds per batch; only what is
still refused then is dropped and counted here. Meanwhile new queries wait in
the worker's UDP socket. A queue that used up that time (a router that is
stopped or hung) is tried once per batch, without waiting, until a batch goes
whole
.TP 8
\fBipc.worker.reply.retried\fR
replies that the queue to the router refused for a moment and took when the
worker sent them again: not a loss, not in \fBipc.dropped.total\fR. On a host
with one CPU it counts the moments the worker had to give the CPU to the router
.TP 8
\fBipc.worker.async.dropped_full\fR
queries that a worker dropped because the queue of the asynchronous resolution
threads (the queries for program zones and forward zones) was full, or no memory
was left to copy the query
.TP 8
\fBipc.worker.udp.truncated\fR, \fBipc.worker.udp.short\fR
datagrams that a worker read from its own UDP socket (\fBudp\-receive
backend\fR) and dropped: larger than its receive buffer (\fBMSG_TRUNC\fR), or
shorter than a DNS header; the counterparts of \fBipc.router.rx.*\fR
.TP 8
\fBipc.router.reply.cut\fR
datagrams of a worker that the router stopped reading at a bad frame that was
not their first one (the frame itself is in \fBshort\fR or \fBbad_length\fR; the
replies after it in that datagram are lost and their number is not known). A
datagram, not a reply, so not in \fBipc.dropped.total\fR. It stays 0 unless the
Backend sends something broken
.TP 8
\fBipc.dropped.total\fR
the sum of all of them but \fBspilled\fR, \fBcut\fR and the two \fBretried\fR
.PP
After the totals come the queries that each Frontend router read from its UDP
sockets and handed to the workers, as lines \fBipcrouter.<n>.queries=<number>\fR
(one per router, from 0; a query that every worker's queue refused is counted
here and, as a loss, in \fBipc.router.worker.dropped_full\fR). They are not
losses and are not in \fBipc.dropped.total\fR. The kernel hands a client's
queries to a router by the client's address and port, so a router that stays at
0 while the others count is one that no client reached: a few clients that all
send from one source port reach one router, however many routers there are.
.PP
With \fBudp\-receive backend\fR (the default, see \fBkaridns(8)\fR) the workers
read the queries from their own UDP sockets, and the lines
\fBipcworker.<n>.queries=<number>\fR (one per worker, from 0) count them; the
router lines then count only what came in on the routers' NOTIFY sockets. The
kernel shares the clients out over the workers by their address and port, as it
does over the routers with \fBudp\-receive frontend\fR (no \fBipcworker\fR lines
then). A reply still goes from the worker to a router (worker \fIn\fR to router
\fIn\fR mod \fIrouters\fR), so \fBipc.worker.reply.*\fR and
\fBipc.router.client.*\fR count the replies that were lost on the way;
\fBipc.router.worker.*\fR and \fBipc.worker.rx.*\fR stay at 0 but for the NOTIFY
sockets. A datagram that the kernel drops at a full UDP socket of a worker (the
worker did not read it in time) is in \fBnetstat \-s \-p udp\fR, as for a
router's.
.PP
After the counters come the sizes of the socket buffers in force, as lines
\fBipcbuf.<kind>.<direction>.asked=<bytes>\fR and \fB.got=<bytes>\fR:
\fBqueue\fR is the queues between the routers and the workers, \fBudp\fR the UDP
sockets of the routers, \fBrcv\fR and \fBsnd\fR the direction; \fBasked\fR is
the largest size requested (\fBudp\-recvbuf\-size\fR, \fBudp\-sndbuf\-size\fR),
\fBgot\fR the smallest size any socket of the kind has (0: not set yet; a UDP
socket is set when its router has started). A \fBgot\fR below \fBasked\fR means
the kernel limit \fBkern.ipc.maxsockbuf\fR held the request down (the server
asks for the most the kernel takes; the start-up \fBWARNING\fR says which value
to set).
.PP
A queue is full when the worker (or the router) does not read it as fast as it
is filled: a CPU that is busy with something else, or buffers that are too
small. The size of the queues is \fBudp\-recvbuf\-size\fR /
\fBudp\-sndbuf\-size\fR; when the kernel did not give the size asked for the
server says so at start-up (\fB[IPC] WARNING\fR, see \fBudp\-recvbuf\-size\fR in
\fBkaridns(8)\fR) and the sysctls \fBkern.ipc.maxsockbuf\fR,
\fBnet.local.dgram.recvspace\fR and \fBnet.local.dgram.maxdgram\fR are the ones
to raise. A datagram that the kernel drops at a full UDP socket of the router
(before the router has read it) is in \fBnetstat \-s \-p udp\fR ("dropped due to
full socket buffers"), not here. Every drop is also logged, once a second at the
most (\fB[Frontend\->Backend IPC] dropped N UDP message(s): ...\fR).
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karictl ipcstats
karictl ipcstats | grep \-v '=0$'
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP 8
\fBdbstatus [zone [view]]\fR
Show the state of the zones served from a database (\fBoptions { database
{ ... }; }\fR, SQLite3 or PostgreSQL through \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR). Without
arguments: whether the loader is connected (with its process ID, version and
drivers, and since when), how many DB zones the server holds (served, hidden by
a zone of the same name in \fBkaridns.conf\fR, hidden by a catalog member of the
same name, and zones with a rejected version), the last batch the loader
delivered (id, kind \fBstartup\fR / \fBfull\fR / \fBdiff\fR / \fBzone\fR /
\fBauto\fR; a \fBreload\fR is a \fBdiff\fR batch, a \fBreload <zone>\fR a
\fBzone\fR batch, time, and its applied / rejected / shadowed / deleted counts),
the state of auto reload (\fBauto reload: off\fR, or \fBon\fR with
poll-interval, coalesce, the number of auto batches since the start and, per
view, the last poll, the applied change log position, the pending changes and
the last error; B9; under a view a line \fBwaiting for the signer: N zone(s): a,
b, ...\fR when zones with the metadata \fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fR = \fB1\fR
changed and wait for \fBkaridns\-signer\fR to sign them and ask for a reload,
see \fBsigner\-wait\fR in \fBkaridns(8)\fR; 0.7, the zone store: a line
\fBsigner\-wait: since the start N version(s) waited for the signer ... (K kept
by the version in memory alone; C held across a start of the loader or the
server), M version(s) served that the signer has not signed yet ...; held in
memory now: Z zone(s), B bytes (counted against memory\-limit)\fR once a version
was held or served unsigned (also without the store since 0.7), and with a zone
named, the lines \fBsigner\-wait: the database has another version, which waits
for the signer; ... keeps serving ...\fR and \fBsigner\-wait: serving a version
the signer has not signed yet ...\fR), for a PostgreSQL view whose schema has
the multi-server tables (B11) one line \fBreload requests (view <name>):
server\-id <id>, last applied generation <n>, ok yes|no, status written
<time>|never, last error: <text>|none\fR (the id this server reports under in
\fBkaridns_server_status\fR, the highest \fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR
generation it has applied, whether every zone of that reload loaded, when its
status row was last written, and why a read of the requests or a write of the
status row failed; the server keeps serving when it cannot write the row), the
size of the zone versions and IXFR deltas (the same numbers as in \fBstatus\fR),
the fetches of whole zones from the database (\fBfetch: rate=unlimited|N/s
refused_by_rate=N issued=N concurrency=N\fR; \fBrate=unlimited\fR is the default
(\fBfetch\-rate\fR, 0.7); \fBrefused_by_rate\fR counts only the background
requests that found the bucket short (a query waits for a token); \fBissued\fR
counts the zones the Backend asked the loader for: a query, a transfer, the
warm-up), then \fBfetchq: in_flight=N places=N peak=N table_full=N
ring_dropped=N park_full=N park_timeout=N loader_busy=N ingest_busy=P%
fill_busy=P% hist=A,B,C,D,E queued=N queue_peak=N waited=N queue_dropped=N
queue_full=N\fR (0.7: where a fetch is held up; all counted since the start
except \fBin_flight\fR, \fBqueued\fR and \fBingest_busy\fR: \fBin_flight\fR and
\fBpeak\fR are the fetches on their way now and the most there have been at one
time; \fBplaces\fR, the places of \fBfetch\-concurrency\fR in use now
(whole-zone fetches in flight: 0 at rest); \fBqueued\fR, \fBqueue_peak\fR and
\fBwaited\fR, the requests that wait now for a place of
\fBfetch\-concurrency\fR, a token of \fBfetch\-rate\fR or room under
\fBmemory\-limit\fR (they are not refused: they wait in order, up to
\fBpark\-timeout\fR), the most there have been, and how many had to wait;
\fBqueue_dropped\fR, those that ran out of \fBpark\-timeout\fR;
\fBqueue_full\fR, those refused because the queue of 8192 was full;
\fBtable_full\fR, requests refused because the 256 places of the fetch table
were all taken; \fBring_dropped\fR, requests the workers could not hand to the
ingest thread because their ring of 128 was full; \fBpark_full\fR, queries that
found the table of parked queries full (\fBpark\-max\fR); \fBpark_timeout\fR,
parked queries whose zone was not there before \fBpark\-timeout\fR;
\fBloader_busy\fR, fetches (and lookups) the loader turned away because its
queue was full (the queries that wait for them are dropped and counted,
\fBstore.park.drop.queue\fR and \fBstore.lookup.drop.queue\fR, not answered
SERVFAIL: busy is overload); \fBingest_busy\fR, the share of the last ten
seconds the ingest thread was working instead of waiting, 0 for the first ten
seconds; \fBfill_busy\fR, the part of that the bodies of the first fill (the
background fetch of the zones the store lists without a body) took; \fBhist\fR,
the time from the request to the install of the fetches that succeeded: up to 1
ms, up to 10 ms, up to 100 ms, up to 1 s, more; see "\fBkarictl dbstatus\fR
shows it" under "Memory limit (D1a)" in \fBkaridns(8)\fR), then (0.7, the first
fill of the zone store) \fBlookup of names while the first pass has not listed
every zone: ready=yes|no in_flight=N found=N none=N failed=N refused=N
same_name=N\fR (the by-name lookups of queries for a name that no stored zone
covers while a view's first pass has not listed every zone, see "The first fill
and the queries (0.7)" in \fBkaridns(8)\fR: \fBready\fR, the store protocol is
on and the loader answers lookups, which is when a query is parked for one;
\fBin_flight\fR, the lookups out now (at most 32, counted from the worker's
request to the end of the ingest thread's work on it); all the others are
counted since the start: \fBfound\fR, lookups that gave a zone, which is then
known (its query is answered from it); \fBnone\fR, lookups that found no zone of
the view that encloses the name (the query is REFUSED, as for any name the
server does not serve); \fBfailed\fR, lookups that ended in a failure (the
loader or its fetch stream went away, the loader named a zone that does not
enclose the name, the zone could not be recorded, or the zone the lookup found
could not be read from the database (the body that came with it failed); each is
logged as \fB[DB] lookup of the name ... failed\fR, and the query is answered
SERVFAIL + EDE 14, not refused; a loader that said it is busy is overload and
the query is dropped, see \fBstore.lookup.drop.queue\fR); \fBrefused\fR,
requests of the workers that the ingest thread did not send because there is
nothing to ask (a name the stream does not take, a view that takes no database
zones, a loader that does not look names up; a loader that is only away does not
count: the lookup waits for it and is dropped at its time); \fBsame_name\fR,
requests that joined a lookup of the same name that was on its way), with the
zone store the state of its journal (0.7: \fBstore: journal=ok|nospace|failed
error=N failed_at=T unwritten=N batch_commit_errors=N\fR, see
\fBstore.journal.state\fR under \fBstore\fR), the NOTIFY of zones that are out
of memory (D1a: \fBnotify of zones out of memory: queued=N deferred=N\fR, the
zones whose serial changed while their data was out of memory that were handed
to the control thread, and the ones that did not fit in the ring of 1024 and are
queued later), the zone transfers of zones that are out of memory (D1a,
\fBtransfers of zones out of memory: waited=N loaded=N failed=N
summary_answers=N marked=N timeout=Nms\fR: transfers that had to wait for the
zone, the zones they got, the ones that ended with SERVFAIL, the IXFRs answered
from the summary without reading the zone, the zones marked evict-next, and
\fBtransfer\-fetch\-timeout\fR) and the cost of the zone directory (\fBzone
directory: N elements, B bytes in the registry + I bytes in the indexes of the
views (P bytes per zone)\fR; the entries and images are not in it), then
\fBalias: scans=N\fR (0.7: how many times the thread that resolves the targets
of ALIAS records scanned the zone directory for them since the start; a scan
follows a zone with ALIAS records coming, changing, going or being taken out of
memory and a reconfiguration, not every zone that is fetched or evicted, so the
number stays where it is while zones without ALIAS records come and go; see
"Expansion" under POWERDNS ALIAS AND LUA RECORDS in \fBkaridns(8)\fR). The
warm-up and the cache (D1a step 7): \fBresidency: directory=N resident=N
out_of_memory=N evictions=N\fR (the zones of the directory with and without data
in memory), \fBqueries: answered=N parked=N hit_rate=P%\fR (the queries the
zones answered, and the ones that had to wait for their zone; the hit rate is
the share answered by a zone already in memory), \fBwarm: received=0|1 queued=N
loaded=N skipped=N (memory limit N, already in memory or gone N) failed=N
pending=N in_flight=N\fR (the zones of the loader's hot list: queued to read,
read, not read because the memory limit's high water mark was reached or because
they were in memory or gone, failed, still waiting, being read),
\fBwarm\-before\-serve: off\fR, \fBholding the queries (N ms left)\fR or
\fBqueries accepted after N ms\fR (the queries are held while the hot list is
read; the control channel is not), and \fBstate\-file: <path>
at_start=... hot_lists_sent=N ... acks: written=N unchanged=N failed=N ...\fR
(what the loader found in its state file at its start, how many hot lists the
Backend sent, and what the loader answered; \fBstate\-file: none\fR without
\fBloader\-state\-file\fR). The line \fBmemory:\fR (0.7) says what the database
needs in memory and what \fBmemory\-limit\fR does about it: \fBmemory: zones=N
(how the size of a zone was found) all_resident: zone_data=X backend=X loader=X
| memory\-limit=L fit=P% backend=X loader=X | recommended=R (half of the zones:
at least H) | status=ok|notice|warning|error\fR; \fBzone_data\fR is what
\fBmemory\-limit\fR would count with every zone in memory, \fBbackend\fR and
\fBloader\fR the resident sizes of the processes (\fBbackend\fR after
\fBall_resident:\fR with every zone in memory, after \fBmemory\-limit=\fR under
the limit), \fBfit\fR the share of the zones that fit under the limit,
\fBrecommended\fR the \fBmemory\-limit\fR that keeps every zone, \fBstatus\fR
the level of the syslog line the server says about it (\fBnotice\fR: below what
every zone needs, \fBwarning\fR: less than half of the zones fit, \fBerror\fR:
the limit cannot hold the zones of the reads in flight). Without
\fBmemory\-limit\fR: \fB| no memory\-limit | recommended=R | status=ok\fR. The
same figures are in syslog at every start and reload (docs/karidns.md, "Memory
limit"). These lines come after the \fBzone directory:\fR line; the lines before
keep their form. Then, the totals since the server started, and the last
protocol error of the loader link. With a zone name (and optionally a view):
that zone's view, database id, SOA serial, status (\fBapplied\fR, \fBrejected\fR
or \fBshadowed\fR) and the last error, then, for a \fBSLAVE\fR or \fBCONSUMER\fR
zone (B7b), its type, masters and TSIG key and the last successful transfer with
the serial (with the zone store, 0.7, \fBa copy is kept in the zone store; a
restart restores it\fR, without it \fBkept in memory only; a restart transfers
it again\fR; \fBsettings from the primary: in force\fR when the primary sent
settings, B8t-2) and (0.7) the lines \fBStore:\fR, \fBLast\-refresh:\fR and
\fBExpired:\fR of \fBzonestatus\fR, indented, then (B7a) the settings it runs
with: where they come from (\fBprofile 'name'\fR with the zone's own metadata
overriding it, \fBthe zone's own metadata\fR, or \fBnone\fR), its
\fBallow\-transfer\fR and \fBallow\-update\fR entries with their match mode, the
entries \fBacl\-ceiling\fR cuts away (\fBlies outside acl\-ceiling\fR, \fBis
narrowed by acl\-ceiling\fR, or \fBno acl\-ceiling is configured: these ACLs
refuse everybody\fR) and a \fBsettings error\fR line when a TSIG key the
settings name is no longer defined in \fBkaridns.conf\fR. The summary also
counts the distinct settings (zones with the same settings share one block) and
the zones that name an undefined key. A zone that was rejected keeps serving its
last good version (its serial is shown). Without a \fBdatabase\fR block the
server answers \fBERROR no database block is configured\fR. With the zone store
(0.7) the \fBstore pass:\fR line shows the reconcile with the loader: the pass
that runs or ran last (the entries it listed, new / changed / renamed /
unchanged / already asked for / refused with this version), the sweep (zones the
pass did not list, deleted), the bodies (records without one now, fetched,
failed, in flight) and the changes applied; a \fBMASS\-DELETE GUARD HELD:\fR
line says that a sweep was held back (more than 20 percent of a view's zones and
more than 1,000 are not in the database) and that \fBkarictl reload \-force\fR
releases it.
.RS 8
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karictl dbstatus
karictl dbstatus example.test
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP 8
\fBstore [\-j | \-k] [zone <name> [view]]\fR
Show the zone store of the database zones (0.7): its state, the zones in it, the
zones in memory, how often zones were attached and how fast, the queries that
had to wait for a zone ("cold" queries), the second-level cache and the memory
advice. Every figure is a counter, a value kept where it changes, or a bounded
sample: a request never walks the zones, and a poll costs the same at 10 million
zones as at a hundred, so it is safe once a second. Reading never resets a
counter. \fBstore\fR needs the same permission as \fBdbstatus\fR (a
\fBread\-only yes\fR key has it).
.RS 8
.PP
Three forms of the same figures: the default is text in sections for a person;
\fB\-j\fR is one JSON object; \fB\-k\fR is one \fBkey=value\fR line per figure
for shell scripts. WebAdmin and the dnstap-live dashboard poll the JSON
form. \fBzone <name> [view]\fR shows the record of one zone instead: one index
lookup, the zone is not attached (a name that exists in several views needs the
view; case and a trailing dot do not matter). Without a store in the
configuration, or before the Backend has opened it, the command still works:
\fBstore.state\fR is \fBnone\fR, and the attach counters and the memory figures
are there.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karictl store
karictl store \-j
karictl store \-k | grep '^store.attach.p99_us='
karictl store zone example.test
karictl store \-j zone example.test external
.fi
.RE
.PP
On ZFS the server cannot read the ARC statistics (it runs in a sandbox);
\fBstore.cache.kind\fR is \fBarc\fR, and \fBkarictl\fR itself appends the
\fBstore.cache.arc.*\fR keys from \fBkstat.zfs.misc.arcstats.*\fR. On UFS the
share of segment pages that are in memory is a \fBmincore(2)\fR sample of at
most 4096 random pages, taken at most every 10 seconds; where \fBmincore\fR is
not allowed \fBstore.cache.page.available\fR is 0 and
\fBstore.cache.page.note\fR says \fBn/a\fR and why.
.PP
\fBThe names of the keys are an interface.\fR They are flat and dotted, the same
in the \fB\-j\fR and the \fB\-k\fR form; in JSON every number is a number (an
integer, never a string, never a float) and every other value a
string. \fBschema\fR is the version of the list (now 1): a key is never renamed
or given another unit within a schema, new keys can appear at any time, so a
reader takes the keys it knows and ignores the others. Names ending in
\fB_bytes\fR are bytes, \fB_us\fR microseconds, \fB_pct\fR an integer
percentage; a latency percentile is the upper bound of the bucket it falls in
(the buckets are 1, 2, 4, ... 8388608 microseconds, so it is exact to a factor
of 2; \fBstore.attach.p*_us\fR and \fBstore.cold.p*_us\fR come from buckets an
eighth of a power of two wide, exact to an eighth, 0.7); \fBmax_us\fR is
exact. A rate is the difference of a counter between two polls divided by the
difference of \fBstore.time.mono_ms\fR (the server's monotonic clock in
milliseconds; \fBstore.time.unix\fR is the wall clock in seconds).
.TP 8
\fBschema\fR
Version of this key list (1)
.TP 8
\fBstore.time.mono_ms\fR, \fBstore.time.unix\fR
Server's monotonic clock in milliseconds, wall clock in seconds, at the moment
of the reply
.TP 8
\fBstore.state\fR
\fBnone\fR (no store), \fBok\fR, or \fBdegraded\fR (the disk is full: new and
changed zones are kept without an image until room is back; \fBstore.space.*\fR)
.TP 8
\fBstore.state_bits\fR
The state as bits: 1 disk full (degraded, or the last write failed for lack of
room), 2 repaired at the last open
.TP 8
\fBstore.format\fR, \fBstore.mode\fR, \fBstore.fstype\fR, \fBstore.uuid\fR
Store format version; \fBmap\fR (segments mapped) or \fBcopy\fR (read with
\fBpread\fR, for ZFS); file system type; store identity (32 hex digits). With
\fBstore.state\fR \fBnone\fR this and every key down to \fBstore.cache.*\fR is
absent
.TP 8
\fBstore.clean_flag\fR
1 when the superblock on disk says "clean stop" (0 while the server runs)
.TP 8
\fBstore.zones.known\fR, \fB.stored\fR, \fB.rejected\fR, \fB.free\fR
Zones in the store by state: known without an image (the database fetch has
them), stored (an image is in the store), rejected (flagged by the last
reconcile), free table slots
.TP 8
\fBstore.zones.nonstore\fR
Zones of the database whose name the store cannot hold (the root zone): they
have no record and are served from the heap directory (with the store protocol;
the line is there when a loader is running)
.TP 8
\fBstore.zones.attached\fR
Zones attached (in memory as images) now: successful attaches minus detaches
.TP 8
\fBstore.zones.image_bytes\fR
Bytes of the stored images (records)
.TP 8
\fBstore.views.count\fR
Views in the store
.TP 8
\fBstore.view.<n>.name\fR, \fB.known\fR, \fB.stored\fR, \fB.rejected\fR, \fB.image_bytes\fR
The same per view, \fB<n>\fR = 0, 1, ... (the store's view number)
.TP 8
\fBstore.tables.zt_records\fR, \fB.zt_capacity\fR, \fB.nx_used_bytes\fR, \fB.nx_dead_bytes\fR
Zone table records in use and room; long names, used and dead
.TP 8
\fBstore.tables.ix_gen\fR, \fB.ix_slots\fR, \fB.ix_used\fR, \fB.ix_live\fR, \fB.sx_gen\fR, \fB.sx_slots\fR, \fB.sx_used\fR, \fB.sx_live\fR
Name index and source index: generation, slots, slots used, live entries
.TP 8
\fBstore.segments.count\fR, \fB.used_bytes\fR, \fB.live_bytes\fR, \fB.dead_pct\fR
Image segments: highest segment number in use, bytes appended to the segments
that exist, bytes still referenced, share that the cleaner can win
.TP 8
\fBstore.segments.free_numbers\fR, \fB.draining\fR, \fB.pinned\fR
Segment numbers that are free (the segment was cleaned and removed; the number
is used again), segments with no live record that wait for a checkpoint, a grace
period or the end of the references, segments that an attached zone's image
points into (MAP mode)
.TP 8
\fBstore.journal.lsn\fR, \fB.checkpoint_lsn\fR, \fB.committed_lsn\fR, \fB.bytes\fR, \fB.gen\fR, \fB.max_bytes\fR
Journal position, last checkpoint, last durable commit, size, generation, the
size at which a checkpoint is forced (100 MiB: a replay of that takes about a
second; 400 MiB while a first fill runs, see \fBstore.journal.boost\fR)
.TP 8
\fBstore.journal.bound_bytes\fR, \fB.peak_bytes\fR, \fB.limit_waits\fR, \fB.limit_wait_us\fR, \fB.limit_wait_max_us\fR
0.7: the bound of the journal (twice \fB.max_bytes\fR), the largest size the
journal (every file since the last checkpoint) reached since the open, and the
writes that waited at that bound for a checkpoint (their number, total and
longest wait). When a checkpoint takes longer than the writing of
\fB.max_bytes\fR of records (the disk does not keep up), the pass of the loader
(the reconcile thread) waits there for it between two groups of entries, with no
lock held, and the thread that also answers lookups waits only at four times
\fB.max_bytes\fR: the journal, the replay after a crash and the clean stop stay
bounded; a WARNING at most once a minute. \fB.limit_waits\fR stays 0 when the
disk keeps up
.TP 8
\fBstore.journal.boost\fR, \fB.boosts\fR
0.7: \fB.boost\fR is 1 while a first fill (the manifest pass that had to reserve
the tables and the index for the zones the loader announces) asks for the larger
journal, \fB.boosts\fR counts the times it did. The first fill of an empty store
keeps no journal at all (\fBstore.bulk.*\fR): the boost is for a pass that
reserves for a store that holds zones already. Every checkpoint of such a pass
writes the whole reserved index (10M zones: 430 to 600 MB), so a checkpoint
every 100 MiB of journal kept the disk busy for the whole pass; while the fill
runs a checkpoint is forced at 400 MiB instead (a fourth of them), and the bound
of the journal (\fB.bound_bytes\fR) follows (800 MiB for the pass, 1.6 GiB for
the thread that also answers lookups). \fB.max_bytes\fR shows the size in force:
the larger one is used only while an eighth of the free space of the file system
covers the journal at its hard bound (12.5 GiB free at the default), and an
unknown free space never raises it; a disk that fills during the fill takes it
back at the next checkpoint. At the end of the pass, or when it is cut off or
replaced by the next one, the size is the normal one again and the checkpoint
that cuts the journal is begun at once. A clean stop is not affected (its
checkpoint is clean whatever the journal holds); a crash during the first fill
replays up to 400 MiB (about 4 s) instead of 100 MiB, a crash after it replays
what it did before
.TP 8
\fBstore.journal.bytes_written\fR
0.7: the bytes the journal files took since the start (every record and the
header of every file): what the journal wrote to the device. It stays at the
headers of a few files while an unlogged first fill runs (\fBstore.bulk.*\fR)
.TP 8
\fBstore.bulk.active\fR, \fB.fills\fR, \fB.unlogged_records\fR, \fB.checkpoints\fR, \fB.next_checkpoint_bytes\fR, \fB.since_unix\fR, \fB.ended_unix\fR
0.7: the first fill of an empty store is unlogged (see "The first fill without a
journal" under the zone store in karidns.md). \fB.active\fR is 1 while it runs,
2 once the passes of every view have listed their zones (it ends when the fill
has nothing more to fetch), 0 otherwise; \fB.fills\fR counts the fills that
began since the start, \fB.unlogged_records\fR the changes made without a
journal record; \fB.checkpoints\fR the bulk checkpoints (the durable points of
the fill: made while the bodies are fetched, when the segments have doubled
since the last one and grew by 4 GiB or more, or 15 minutes after the last one),
\fB.next_checkpoint_bytes\fR the size of the segments at which the next one is
due (0 while a pass runs); when the fill began (or was resumed) and when the
last one ended
.TP 8
\fBstore.bulk.resumed\fR, \fB.recoveries\fR, \fB.recovered_known\fR
0.7: the start found an unlogged fill going on (1) and went on with it;
\fB.recoveries\fR is 1 when the server had not stopped cleanly (a crash or kill
\-9 cut the fill off: nothing was replayed, the store went back to its last bulk
checkpoint, or to the start of the fill, and rebuilt its tables; the cursors of
the views were dropped, so the loader lists every zone again),
\fB.recovered_known\fR the zones whose image was past that checkpoint: kept as
known zones with their name and stamp, fetched again. A clean stop in the middle
of a fill needs no rebuild: \fB.resumed\fR is 1 and \fB.recoveries\fR 0
.TP 8
\fBstore.bulk.end_pending\fR, \fB.cut\fR
0.7: \fB.end_pending\fR is 1 from the end of an unlogged fill until the
checkpoint that clears it on disk is done (the store's checkpoint thread makes
it, and tries again after a failure with a back-off of 1 s doubling up to 5 min,
not at all while the free space is below what it writes; the cleaner, the
removal of segments and the scrub stay off until then, and a crash meanwhile is
a crash of the fill); \fB.cut\fR is 1 from the start that found a fill cut off
by a crash until a start with the database has dropped the cursors of the views
(kept on disk across a start with no database block)
.TP 8
\fBstore.checkpoint.count\fR, \fB.last_unix\fR, \fB.last_us\fR, \fB.max_us\fR, \fB.cancelled\fR
Checkpoints since the open, when the last one ended, how long it took, the
longest one, and the checkpoints that a stop left in the middle (its clean
checkpoint flushed everything instead). The write of the tables runs outside the
store's writer mutex: writers go on meanwhile
.TP 8
\fBstore.checkpoint.table_bytes\fR, \fB.table_last_bytes\fR
0.7: the bytes of the tables (\fBzt\fR, \fBzc\fR, \fBnx\fR, \fBix\fR, \fBsx\fR)
that checkpoints wrote since the open, and the last one: a checkpoint copies the
pages that changed since the one before and writes the copy, so the writers
never wait for a page that is being written out (\fB.paced_us\fR: the time a
checkpoint waited because it had written more than 256 MiB a second, so that the
writers' journal appends do not queue behind a burst of it)
.TP 8
\fBstore.tables.private_bytes\fR
0.7: the pages of the tables that the server changed since it started: memory of
the process that the kernel cannot drop (about 200 bytes a zone once every page
was written; 0 after a restart until a zone changes). \fBmemory\-limit\fR counts
it: the zones in memory get the limit less this, at least a tenth of the limit
(docs/karidns.md, "The tables in memory")
.TP 8
\fBstore.writes.commits\fR, \fB.puts\fR, \fB.replaces\fR, \fB.deletes\fR, \fB.growths\fR, \fB.errors\fR
Writer counters since the open (\fBerrors\fR counts a commit or a checkpoint
that failed in the background too; it is tried again)
.TP 8
\fBstore.writes.names_refused\fR
0.7: writer calls refused because the zone's name holds a zero byte (no writer
of the server makes such a name; a recovery would take one for a page of the
long names that did not reach the disk). Should stay 0
.TP 8
\fBstore.reserve.zones\fR, \fB.nx_bytes\fR, \fB.zones_used\fR, \fB.nx_used\fR, \fB.refused\fR
0.7: the address space the tables of the store are reserved for until the next
start (\fBstore\-max\-zones\fR, or the RAM; see "Address space of the tables"
under the zone store in karidns.md): the zones \fBzt\fR and \fBzc\fR have room
for and the bytes of \fBnx\fR, what is used of them (the highest zone id, the
bytes of long names), and the writes refused because a table was full (the zone
is served from the database; an ERROR names
\fBstore\-max\-zones\fR). \fB.refused\fR should stay 0; a WARNING says when a
table is over 80 %
.TP 8
\fBstore.journal.commit_switches\fR
0.7: commits that switched the journal to the next file (made ahead): the
records of a commit are synced in their file while the writers append to the
next one, so an append never waits for the sync of its file (UFS holds the
file's lock for a sync). A file goes once neither superblock names the chain it
is in
.TP 8
\fBstore.journal.state\fR, \fB.error\fR, \fB.failed_at_unix\fR
0.7: the write of the journal, which is made after the writer mutex (see "The
journal ring" under the zone store in karidns.md): \fBok\fR; \fBnospace\fR (a
write met ENOSPC: new records and every wait for the journal get "no space" at
once, the flusher writes again every 0.1 to 1 s until there is room, then
\fBok\fR; a WARNING once a minute); \fBfailed\fR (a write failed three times
with another error, EIO: the store takes no more records, writes no table page
and makes no clean checkpoint at the stop; an ERROR once a minute; restart the
server, the open replays what was written). \fB.error\fR is the errno of the
last failed write, \fB.failed_at_unix\fR when the state became \fBfailed\fR
.TP 8
\fBstore.journal.ring_bytes\fR, \fB.ring_unwritten_bytes\fR, \fB.ring_peak_bytes\fR
0.7: the memory of the journal ring (the records wait there for their write: at
most 64 MiB, counted in \fBmemory\-limit\fR), what waits now, and the most that
waited since the start
.TP 8
\fBstore.journal.ring_waits\fR, \fB.ring_wait_max_us\fR, \fB.ring_full_in_lock\fR
0.7: writer calls that waited before the writer mutex because the ring was
nearly full (a write that stalls for seconds), the longest such wait, and the
reservations that found it full inside the mutex and wrote it there (the old
behaviour; should stay 0)
.TP 8
\fBstore.journal.leader_writes\fR, \fB.bytes_per_write\fR, \fB.write_max_us\fR, \fB.retries\fR, \fB.ck_waits\fR
0.7: the \fBpwrite()\fR calls that wrote the ring (a writer, or the flusher,
writes every record that waits, in order), their mean size, the longest one, the
writes tried again after an error, and the runs of table pages a checkpoint held
until the journal was written up to them (a checkpoint never writes a table page
whose change the kernel's journal does not hold yet)
.TP 8
\fBstore.journal.defer_busy\fR
0.7: calls of a thread that wanted to defer (not wait for its journal records)
and found no free slot: they waited instead. Should stay 0
.TP 8
\fBstore.writes.prewrites\fR, \fB.prewrite_misses\fR
0.7: image records that \fBstore_put()\fR wrote before the writer mutex (the
change inside it only records where they are), and those it wrote again inside
the mutex because the zone, its record or the open segment changed in between. A
new zone and a full segment are written inside the mutex
.TP 8
\fBstore.ahead.allocs\fR, \fB.alloc_us\fR, \fB.alloc_max_us\fR, \fB.thread_max_us\fR, \fB.errors\fR
0.7: the allocation of the blocks ahead of the tables (\fBzt\fR, \fBzc\fR,
\fBnx\fR: \fBposix_fallocate()\fR when what is in use nears the end of what is
mapped, and for \fBstore_reserve()\fR), so that a growth inside the writer mutex
is a mapping: how many were made, their total and longest time in microseconds
(every caller, the one of \fBstore_reserve()\fR too), the longest round of the
thread of its own (\fB.thread_max_us\fR: what the thread waited for the disk,
without the reserve), and how many failed (a full disk or an I/O error: nothing
is done then, the checkpoint that extends the file meets the same error). Since
0.7 the flusher makes them in a thread of its own: a slow disk (up to 11 s were
seen on UFS) no longer stops the periodic commit and the journal ring's
background leader
.TP 8
\fBstore.ahead.busy_kicks\fR, \fB.unallocated_growths\fR
0.7: how often the commit thread asked for the blocks ahead while that thread
was still in its round before (the work lags behind the writers; the commit
thread went on), and the growths of a table that found its blocks not allocated
yet (the checkpoint allocates them when it extends the file: a longer checkpoint
and a later warning about a full disk, never a wrong answer)
.TP 8
\fBstore.segment.precreated\fR, \fB.inline_creates\fR, \fB.precreate_ready\fR
0.7: the file of the next segment (\fBseg.N\fR) is made ahead by a thread of the
flusher once the open segment is half full, outside every lock, so that the
writer mutex does not wait for the file system to make a 256 MiB file. How many
segments were taken from the file made ahead, how many files were made inside
the writer mutex instead (the first segment, a fill faster than the disk, a
failure of the thread: the old behaviour), and 1 while a file waits
.TP 8
\fBstore.segment.precreate_errors\fR, \fB.precreate_us\fR, \fB.precreate_max_us\fR, \fB.inline_create_max_us\fR
0.7: files the thread could not make (the writer makes it then and meets the
error itself), the time the thread took for its files (total and longest, no
lock held), and the longest file made inside the writer mutex (the part of
\fBstore.lock.writer_hold_max_us\fR that a segment file made)
.TP 8
\fBstore.writes.growths_background\fR
Of \fBgrowths\fR: the index growths that were built outside the writer mutex (a
growth is made inside it only when the index fills up to one half before the
build ends). The changes the writers made to the index while it was built are
made on the new one with no lock too, in rounds (0.7); only the last few
thousand of them are made inside the mutex, with the record that puts the new
index in
.TP 8
\fBstore.index.waits\fR, \fB.wait_us\fR, \fB.wait_max_us\fR, \fB.wait_giveups\fR
0.7: the waits of the reconcile thread (the pass of the loader) for the build of
the next index generation. A build begins at 3/8 of the index; when the index is
past 7/16 before it ends, the pass waits between two groups of entries, with no
lock held, instead of filling the old index to one half, where the growth would
be made inside the writer mutex (the build dropped, every zone put in again:
seconds at millions of zones). The waits (their number, total and longest time)
and those that ended at their bound (30 s) with the build still running: the
pass goes on then and the growth inside the mutex is the last resort (a
WARNING). A first fill that announces its size (the loader's estimate, up to a
quarter of \fBmemory\-limit\fR) makes its index once and waits for nothing
.TP 8
\fBstore.flusher\fR
1: the store's flusher threads make the commits (every 100 ms while records
wait) and the checkpoints; 0: the writers make them after their call (no thread
could be started)
.TP 8
\fBstore.lock.writer_holds\fR, \fB.writer_holds_slow\fR, \fB.writer_hold_max_us\fR, \fB.writer_hold_max_site\fR
Holds of the store's writer mutex since the open, those of 1 ms or more, the
longest one in microseconds, and the function that held it (\fB\-\fR before the
first hold). No sync of the disk is waited for inside it (0.7); a long hold is
CPU work of one call (an nx pass, a repair, a growth inside the mutex; the
memory of a replaced index generation is released after it, 0.7), or a wait of
the disk that another thread causes: a store into a page of the tables waits
while a checkpoint writes that page out, and the append of a journal record
meets the file system's back-pressure (up to 2.7 s were measured in a first fill
of ten million zones under load; 0.7 no longer appends to a file that a sync
holds: see \fB.journal.commit_switches\fR and \fBstore.writes.prewrites\fR). A
hold of a second or more is also logged (at most once a minute) with its
function
.TP 8
\fBstore.lock.site.<function>.slow\fR, \fB.over_10ms\fR, \fB.over_100ms\fR, \fB.slow_us\fR, \fB.max_us\fR
The holds of the writer mutex of 1 ms or more by the function that took it
(0.7): how many, how many of 10 ms and of 100 ms or more, their total time and
the longest. A function appears with its first such hold; at most 24 functions
are kept (\fB<function>\fR is cut at 24 characters). The distribution says
whether \fB.writer_hold_max_us\fR was one event or a pattern
.TP 8
\fBstore.lock.records_holds\fR, \fB.records_holds_slow\fR, \fB.records_hold_max_us\fR, \fB.records_wait_max_us\fR
The mutex that orders the writers of the records (the reconcile, the commit of a
body, a lookup's answer): holds since the start, those of 1 ms or more, the
longest hold and the longest wait for it, in microseconds (the line is there
when a loader is running)
.TP 8
\fBstore.lock.writer_hold_p50_us\fR, \fB.writer_hold_p99_us\fR, \fB.writer_hold_p999_us\fR, \fB.records_hold_p999_us\fR
0.7: percentiles of every hold of the writer mutex (and the 99.9th of the
records mutex) since the start, from a histogram of an eighth of an octave
(exact to an eighth, at most the longest hold). The target of 0.7 is a p99.9 of
2 ms
.TP 8
\fBstore.recon.settle_errors\fR, \fB.batch_store_errors\fR, \fB.batch_store_last_error\fR
0.7: the waits for the journal after the records mutex that found it full or
failed (the store calls had returned), the batch ends whose commit failed (the
store's cursor stays where it was durable: the next start reads the changes
again), and the errno of the last one (0: it worked)
.TP 8
\fBstore.recovery.created\fR, \fB.was_clean\fR, \fB.used_older_super\fR, \fB.rebuilt\fR, \fB.journals_read\fR, \fB.records_replayed\fR, \fB.journal_torn\fR, \fB.rebuilt_zones\fR, \fB.dropped_zones\fR, \fB.open_us\fR
What the last open found and did, and how long it took. \fB.rebuilt_zones\fR is
the number of live zones (KNOWN or STORED) the rebuild kept; free cells and
dropped records are not counted, so it is not the high-water mark of the zone
table
.TP 8
\fBstore.open.ix_read_us\fR, \fB.zt_readahead_us\fR, \fB.zt_readahead_done\fR
The open reads the name index once before the first answer (\fBix_read_us\fR:
how long that took, with the wait for the disk for every page that was not in
memory: 17.6 MB of a million zones took 16.6 ms from a cold ARC and 1.4 ms from
a warm one); a thread then reads the zone tables (zt and zc) into memory in the
background (\fBzt_readahead_us\fR, 0 until \fBzt_readahead_done\fR is 1)
.TP 8
\fBstore.errors.total\fR
Texts put into the error ring (\fBerr.ring\fR, the last 65,536 are kept) since
it was made: why a zone was refused or failed
.TP 8
\fBstore.entries.available\fR
1 when the figures of the server's own tables below are there (a server with the
store on)
.TP 8
\fBstore.entries.pool_used\fR, \fB.pool_slabs\fR, \fB.pool_slab_bytes\fR, \fB.pool_slot_bytes\fR, \fB.pool_free_slots\fR
The pool of zone entries: slots in use (one for every zone in memory), slabs,
bytes of a slab, bytes of a slot (256), free slots
.TP 8
\fBstore.entries.attach_table_slots\fR, \fB.attach_table_grows\fR, \fB.attach_table_shrinks\fR
The table that finds a zone in memory by its zone id: slots now, how often it
was made larger and smaller
.TP 8
\fBstore.fold.zones\fR, \fB.rows\fR, \fB.replaced\fR
Zones that left memory with their counters folded into the totals of their view,
rows of the table of the top zones (those with the most queries that are not in
memory), rows a busier zone took over
.TP 8
\fBstore.errors.memory_texts\fR, \fB.memory_noted\fR, \fB.memory_pushed_out\fR
The texts of rejected zones in the ring in memory (512), how many were noted
since the start, how many were pushed out by newer ones (the file \fBerr.ring\fR
still has them; \fBstore.errors.total\fR)
.TP 8
\fBstore.detach.sampled\fR
Zones that were taken out of memory because the sample of the attach table chose
them (also in \fBstore.detach.count\fR)
.TP 8
\fBstore.attach.build_ns_per_zone\fR
0.7: the mean time of the first half of an attach on the store threads (the
record and image read, the checks, the entry made; not the registration), in
nanoseconds, timed for one attach in eight
.TP 8
\fBstore.attach.worker.ok\fR, \fB.mean_us\fR
0.7: zones that a worker attached itself and answered from (MAP mode, the image
in the page cache; also in \fBstore.attach.count\fR), and the mean time that
took in microseconds
.TP 8
\fBstore.attach.worker.not_resident\fR, \fB.over_limit\fR, \fB.claimed\fR, \fB.failed\fR, \fB.declined\fR, \fB.full\fR
Why a worker did not: the pages of the image were not in the page cache (or COPY
mode); above the hard line of \fBmemory\-limit\fR; another thread had the zone;
the image or the entry could not be made (the store threads then look at it and
say why); a name server of the zone lies in a stored zone that is not in memory;
the table of the staged zones or the ring was full
.TP 8
\fBstore.attach.worker.registered\fR, \fB.dropped\fR
Zones that a worker attached and a store thread then registered / found in
memory already, changed or gone (dropped: the answer the worker gave was the
version the record had)
.TP 8
\fBstore.attach.shed\fR, \fB.newest_first\fR, \fB.queued\fR
0.7: the attach requests of the workers that the store threads dropped unread
because nobody waited for them any more (every worker that had a query for the
zone had left the zone's claim: the queries ended at \fBpark\-timeout\fR, or
another request did the zone); the requests taken newest first because the store
threads were behind (the oldest request of a ring was over 400 ms old: the
queries that came last have the most time left; one take in eight is still the
oldest); the slots of the workers' attach rings in use now (waiting requests and
the slots of requests taken out of order). A request that a query still waits
for is never dropped. A \fB.shed\fR that rises with \fBstore.cold.timeout\fR
means more first touches of stored zones are asked for than the store threads
attach (\fBstore\-threads\fR, a larger \fBmemory\-limit\fR)
.TP 8
\fBstore.lookup.available\fR, \fB.ready\fR
0.7, the lookups of the first start (a query for a name that no stored zone
covers while a view's first pass has not listed every zone): 1 when the figures
below are there; 1 when the loader answers lookups
.TP 8
\fBstore.lookup.asked\fR, \fB.found\fR, \fB.none\fR, \fB.failed\fR
Requests of the workers that the ingest thread took; how they ended: a zone was
found and recorded, no zone, a failure (the loader's error, no answer within the
time, a zone that does not enclose the name)
.TP 8
\fBstore.lookup.pending\fR, \fB.pending_peak\fR, \fB.in_flight\fR
Lookups that wait for a place of \fBfetch\-concurrency\fR now, the most that
waited, lookups out now (with the bodies that came with them: at most three
quarters of the places)
.TP 8
\fBstore.lookup.take_fail\fR
How often a worker could not make the request of a lookup because its ring to
the ingest thread was full: the query stays parked as pending and the worker
asks again, it is not refused
.TP 8
\fBstore.lookup.body_inline\fR
Zones whose body came with the answer of the lookup (FETCH_REQ mode 3: one round
trip) and was taken
.TP 8
\fBstore.lookup.bad_body\fR, \fB.refused\fR, \fB.same_name\fR, \fB.covered\fR, \fB.shed\fR, \fB.expired\fR
Bodies thrown away because the answer did not announce them (before the answer,
another zone or id, a second one); requests not made (no loader that looks up, a
view without database zones); the same name already on its way; the zone of the
name was known when it was to go; dropped because the queue was full; dropped
because they waited \fBpark\-timeout\fR
.TP 8
\fBstore.lookup.drop.room\fR, \fB.drop.timeout\fR, \fB.drop.queue\fR, \fB.servfail\fR
0.7: queries that were dropped (nothing is answered, like a query that
\fBrate\-limit\fR drops; a TCP connection stays open and its next message is
read) because the lookup found no room in the worker's park table, its
\fBpark\-timeout\fR was up before the lookup was answered, or the ingest thread
dropped the lookup (its queue was full, or it waited there until its time was
up); and queries answered SERVFAIL + EDE 14 because their lookup failed
.TP 8
\fBstore.lookup.remembered\fR, \fB.reask_suppressed\fR, \fB.remember_releases\fR
0.7: names whose lookup the ingest thread shed (its queue was full, they waited
there until \fBpark\-timeout\fR, or the loader said it was busy) and put in the
table of the shed names; queries for such a name that their worker dropped at
once, with no request, because the request would be shed again (also a drop,
like \fB.drop.*\fR); times the names were forgotten before their time because
the ingest thread had caught up (its queue was empty and a place free; at most
eight times in a \fBpark\-timeout\fR). A name is remembered for
\fBpark\-timeout\fR
.TP 8
\fBstore.lookup.drain.passes\fR, \fB.drain.cut\fR, \fB.drain.pass_max\fR, \fB.drain.pass_max_us\fR
The passes of the ingest thread over the workers' rings and queues that handled
a request (a pass runs about 1 ms and then serves the loader's answers); how
many ended at that time slice with work left; the most requests one pass handled
and the longest one ran (microseconds), since the start. A \fBpass_max\fR of a
few thousand with \fBstore.lookup.take_fail\fR and \fBdrop.room\fR still rising
means the thread does not keep up
.TP 8
\fBstore.park.drop.room\fR, \fB.drop.timeout\fR, \fB.drop.queue\fR
The same for a query that waits for a zone that is out of memory: the park table
was full (\fBpark\-max\fR), its \fBpark\-timeout\fR was up before the zone was
there, the wait queue of the ingest thread was full
.TP 8
\fBstore.lookup.rtt.p50_us\fR, \fB.p99_us\fR, \fB.max_us\fR, \fB.rtt.hist.*\fR
The time from the request to the answer (without the body), as the attach times:
the upper bound of the bucket that holds the percentile, the longest, the
buckets (<= 1 us, <= 2 us, ... in the JSON and \fB\-k\fR forms)
.TP 8
\fBstore.hot.state\fR, \fB.listed\fR, \fB.attached\fR, \fB.skipped\fR, \fB.written_at_stop\fR, \fB.threads\fR, \fB.attached_per_s\fR
0.7: the hot list of the store: \fBnone\fR, \fBattaching\fR, \fBdone\fR,
\fBhigh\-mark\fR (stopped at the high mark of \fBmemory\-limit\fR) or \fBoff\fR
(\fBstore\-hot\-list no;\fR); the ids read at the start; the zones it attached
and the ones it skipped (in memory already, not stored, or that could not be
attached); the ids written at the last stop (this run); the threads of the list
(three quarters of the store threads, at most six; they give the attach requests
of waiting queries at most 0.4 ms before each batch of 32 zones, and only while
the store threads are taking requests in hand) and the zones a second they
attached, from the start of the list to now (to its end once it has ended); the
ids written at the stop are the zones that have answered a query since they came
into memory
.TP 8
\fBstore.compact.runs\fR, \fB.moved_records\fR, \fB.moved_bytes\fR, \fB.segments_freed\fR, \fB.last_unix\fR
The cleaner (since the open): segments it began to clean, records and bytes it
copied to the open segment, segments it removed, when it last worked
.TP 8
\fBstore.compact.bad_records\fR, \fB.pinned_skips\fR, \fB.incomplete\fR
Live records that failed their checksum when the cleaner was to copy them (the
zone became known and is read from the database again), emptied segments that
were held by an attached image (counted once each), segments the cleaner could
not empty (a record it cannot read; it tries again after ten minutes)
.TP 8
\fBstore.compact.rate_bytes\fR, \fB.candidates\fR, \fB.dead_bytes\fR
Bytes a second the cleaner may copy (0 = no limit), segments that are dead
enough (half dead and at least a quarter of a segment or 1 MiB dead) now, dead
bytes of all segments but the open one
.TP 8
\fBstore.compact.nx_reclaims\fR, \fB.nx_reclaimed_bytes\fR
Passes that gave back the space of long zone names that were deleted
(\fBstore.tables.nx_dead_bytes\fR), and the bytes they gave back
.TP 8
\fBstore.space.level\fR
\fBok\fR, \fBlow\fR (a WARNING was said: the file system is getting full),
\fBdegraded\fR (no room: new and changed zones are kept without an image)
.TP 8
\fBstore.space.fs_total_bytes\fR, \fB.fs_free_bytes\fR, \fB.checked_unix\fR
The file system of the store, as of the last check (at every checkpoint, about
every second while images are written)
.TP 8
\fBstore.space.reserve_bytes\fR, \fB.store_bytes\fR
Free bytes that only the cleaner may use (images are refused below that: half a
segment, 256 KiB to 128 MiB); what the store occupies
.TP 8
\fBstore.space.estimate_bytes\fR, \fB.reprobe_in_lock\fR
0.7: the free space the store reckons with between two readings (the last
reading less every byte written or reserved since, the journal ring's unwritten
bytes included), and the readings made inside the store's writer lock to confirm
a refusal (only when the estimate says the call does not fit, i.e. the file
system is nearly full, and at most every 100 ms)
.TP 8
\fBstore.space.growth_bytes_per_hour\fR, \fB.full_in_hours\fR
The growth over the last hour (0 until five minutes of history exist, or when
the store shrinks) and the hours until the file system is full at that growth (0
= not known)
.TP 8
\fBstore.space.degraded_puts\fR, \fB.enospc_events\fR, \fB.degraded_since_unix\fR
Zones that were kept without an image because no room was left (since the open),
writes that found no room, when the degraded state began (0 = not degraded)
.TP 8
\fBstore.fs.blksize\fR, \fB.recordsize_warning\fR
\fBst_blksize\fR of a table file (on ZFS the record size of the dataset) and 1
when it is above 32 KiB (the server says so in syslog; see "The zone store" in
karidns.md)
.TP 8
\fBstore.scrub.interval_s\fR, \fB.running\fR, \fB.progress_pct\fR
Seconds between two scrubs (0 = never), whether a pass runs, how far it is
.TP 8
\fBstore.scrub.passes\fR, \fB.last_start_unix\fR, \fB.last_end_unix\fR, \fB.zones_checked\fR, \fB.bytes_checked\fR
Passes since the open, when the last began and ended, zones and bytes read
.TP 8
\fBstore.scrub.bad_images\fR, \fB.bad_cells\fR, \fB.bad_index\fR, \fB.io_errors\fR
What the scrub found since the open: images whose checksum or structure was
wrong (the zone became known and is read again), damaged table records, index
entries that did not lead to their zone (both repaired at the end of the pass),
read errors
.TP 8
\fBstore.cache.kind\fR
\fBpage\fR (UFS: \fBmincore\fR sample), \fBarc\fR (ZFS), \fBcopy\fR
.TP 8
\fBstore.cache.page.available\fR
1 when the sample worked, else 0 and \fBstore.cache.page.note\fR ("n/a: ...")
.TP 8
\fBstore.cache.page.sampled_pages\fR, \fB.resident_pages\fR, \fB.resident_pct\fR, \fB.sample_age_ms\fR
The sample: pages looked at, of them in memory, the share, age of the sample
.TP 8
\fBstore.cache.note\fR
On ZFS: \fBsee karictl (ARC kstats)\fR
.TP 8
\fBstore.cache.arc.available\fR, \fB.size_bytes\fR, \fB.target_bytes\fR, \fB.target_min_bytes\fR, \fB.target_max_bytes\fR, \fB.hits\fR, \fB.misses\fR, \fB.hit_pct\fR, \fB.compressed_bytes\fR, \fB.uncompressed_bytes\fR
ARC figures, added by \fBkarictl\fR on ZFS (a kstat that cannot be read is left
out)
.TP 8
\fBstore.memory.arc.backend_bytes\fR, \fB.arc_max_bytes\fR, \fB.loader_bytes\fR, \fB.other_bytes\fR, \fB.total_bytes\fR, \fB.ram_bytes\fR, \fB.pct\fR, \fB.level\fR, \fB.text\fR
Added by \fBkarictl\fR on ZFS: the Backend's heap (with \fBmemory\-limit\fR its
size under the limit, else with every zone in memory), \fBvfs.zfs.arc.max\fR,
the loader, about 1 GB for the system, their sum against \fBhw.physmem\fR, the
share in percent, \fBok\fR / \fBnotice\fR (above 85 percent) / \fBwarning\fR
(above RAM) and the sentence (in the text form: the section \fBarc sum\fR)
.TP 8
\fBstore.attach.count\fR
Zones attached from the store since the start
.TP 8
\fBstore.attach.failed\fR
Attaches that failed; \fBstore.attach.fail.checksum\fR, \fB.record\fR,
\fB.invalid\fR, \fB.io\fR, \fB.memory\fR, \fB.other\fR by cause
.TP 8
\fBstore.attach.p50_us\fR, \fB.p90_us\fR, \fB.p99_us\fR, \fB.p999_us\fR, \fB.max_us\fR
Time of an attach: the percentiles from a finer histogram than the buckets below
(0.7: one bucket per microsecond up to 7 us, then eight per power of two), exact
to an eighth (\fB.p999_us\fR is the 99.9th percentile, 0.7)
.TP 8
\fBstore.attach.hist.le_<2^i>us\fR, \fB.hist.more\fR
The histogram: attaches that took at most 1, 2, 4, ... 8388608 microseconds
(each in its own bucket, not cumulative), and longer; not in the text form
.TP 8
\fBstore.detach.count\fR
Zones taken out of memory; \fBstore.detach.limit\fR (memory-limit), \fB.room\fR
(to make room for another zone), \fB.operator\fR, \fB.other\fR by reason
.TP 8
\fBstore.cold.attach\fR, \fB.fetch\fR, \fB.timeout\fR
Queries for a zone that was not attached: answered after an attach, sent to a
database fetch (a zone with no image, or a damaged one: a query that waits for a
fetch or an attach that another query started is not counted), ended by the park
time-out
.TP 8
\fBstore.cold.p50_us\fR, \fB.p90_us\fR, \fB.p99_us\fR, \fB.p999_us\fR, \fB.max_us\fR, \fB.hist.le_<2^i>us\fR, \fB.hist.more\fR
Time from the parked query to its answer, for \fBstore.cold.attach\fR; the
percentiles as for \fBstore.attach.*\fR (exact to an eighth), the buckets of
powers of two
.TP 8
\fBstore.memory.available\fR
1 when the figures below are there
.TP 8
\fBstore.memory.zones\fR, \fB.records\fR, \fB.basis\fR
Zones and records the estimate is for, and where the size of a zone comes from
(\fBassumed\fR, \fBresident\fR, \fBrecords\fR, or \fBstore\fR: the average image
of the zones the store holds)
.TP 8
\fBstore.memory.tables_bytes\fR, \fB.mapped_bytes\fR, \fB.copy_mode\fR
With the store: the size of its tables (file pages, not heap: the directory
costs the heap nothing), every file page the Backend maps for the zones (the
tables, and in \fBmap\fR mode the images of the zones in memory), and 1 for
\fBcopy\fR mode (the images are heap)
.TP 8
\fBstore.memory.counted_all_bytes\fR, \fB.avg_zone_bytes\fR, \fB.backend_all_bytes\fR, \fB.loader_all_bytes\fR, \fB.directory_bytes\fR
What \fBmemory\-limit\fR counts with every zone in memory, the average zone, the
Backend and the loader with every zone in memory, the directory alone
.TP 8
\fBstore.memory.recommended_limit_bytes\fR, \fB.working_min_bytes\fR, \fB.fit_min_bytes\fR, \fB.limit_bytes\fR, \fB.fit_pct\fR, \fB.backend_limit_bytes\fR, \fB.loader_limit_bytes\fR
\fBmemory\-limit\fR that keeps every zone, that keeps half, below which the
server says ERROR, the one in force (0 = none), the share of the zones that fit,
the Backend and the loader under it
.TP 8
\fBstore.memory.level\fR
\fBok\fR, \fBnotice\fR, \fBwarning\fR or \fBerror\fR: what the server says in
syslog about \fBmemory\-limit\fR
.TP 8
\fBstore.recon.available\fR
1 when the figures below are there (the Backend has a loader that speaks the
store protocol)
.TP 8
\fBstore.recon.pass.active\fR, \fB.view\fR, \fB.started_unix\fR, \fB.duration_ms\fR, \fB.estimate\fR
The pass of the loader that is running or was the last: its view, when it
started, how long it took (so far), the entries the loader announced
.TP 8
\fBstore.recon.pass.entries\fR, \fB.created\fR, \fB.changed\fR, \fB.renamed\fR, \fB.same\fR, \fB.pending\fR, \fB.kept\fR, \fB.skipped\fR
What the pass found: zones listed, new, with another stamp, renamed, unchanged,
already asked for, refused with this stamp and not asked for again, and the ones
the loader could not offer (\fBn_skipped\fR)
.TP 8
\fBstore.recon.sweep.total\fR, \fB.unlisted\fR, \fB.deleted\fR
The last sweep: zones of the view in the store, of them not listed by the pass,
deleted
.TP 8
\fBstore.recon.passes\fR, \fB.wanted\fR, \fB.cursors_kept\fR
Since the start: passes, zones whose body was asked for by a pass or a change,
cursors made durable
.TP 8
\fBstore.recon.bodies.known\fR, \fB.fetched\fR, \fB.failed\fR, \fB.in_flight\fR
Records without a body now (known), and what the background fetch has done since
the start: bodies brought, failed, asked for and not here yet
.TP 8
\fBstore.recon.changes.upserts\fR, \fB.deletes\fR
Entries of the change log applied (upserts and deletes)
.TP 8
\fBstore.recon.signer.held\fR, \fB.held_in_memory\fR, \fB.unsigned\fR
0.7 signer-wait, since the start: versions that waited for \fBkaridns\-signer\fR
while a signed one kept serving (the image, or the version in memory); of those,
the ones only the version in memory kept (the record had no image: the scrub or
the cleaner found it damaged in \fBstore\-mode copy\fR, a degraded store kept
only the record), which is then held in memory; versions served although the
signer has not signed them, because nothing signed was here to keep (a zone new
to the server; a held version that had to leave memory): a WARNING names them
and \fBkarictl dbstatus <zone>\fR says "serving a version the signer has not
signed yet" (\fBkaridns(8)\fR, "The signer's zones")
.TP 8
\fBstore.recon.signer.carried\fR
0.7 signer-wait, since the start: holds made before a start of the loader or the
server that the first batch of the loader said again (the zone still waits for
the signer by its state in the database, \fBkaridns_signer_state\fR): the signed
version kept serving across the start (\fBkaridns(8)\fR, "The signer's zones",
"Across a restart")
.TP 8
\fBstore.recon.thread\fR, \fB.busy_pct\fR, \fB.entries_per_s\fR, \fB.queued\fR, \fB.apply_ns_per_entry\fR
0.7, the reconcile thread (the pages of a manifest pass are decoded and recorded
on a thread of their own, not on the one that answers the fetches and the
lookups): 1 when it runs; the share of the last second it was busy, the entries
it applied a second in that second, the pages that wait for it now (a ring of 8;
the thread that reads the stream stops reading when it is full), the wall time
of the apply of an entry (records in groups of up to 32) in the last second that
had entries
.TP 8
\fBstore.recon.thread_entries\fR, \fB.thread_pages\fR, \fB.ring_full_stops\fR
Since the start: entries and pages the thread applied; how often the thread that
reads the stream held a page back because the ring was full (the loader's socket
then fills and the loader waits: the stream is not read, the fetch stream is
served all the time)
.TP 8
\fBstore.recon.group_records\fR, \fB.group_entries\fR
Journal records the store wrote for groups of entries (one record, one hold of
the writer's mutex and one decision of the commit for up to 32 new zones) and
the entries they carried: the entries of a first fill are about 32 to a record,
and a record that is cut off by a crash is dropped whole
.TP 8
\fBstore.ingest.loop_max_us\fR, \fB.stream_max_us\fR
The longest pass of the ingest thread's loop and the longest read of the
loader's first stream (the frames of a pass are copies for the reconcile thread,
so this is short whatever the pass is) in the last 10 to 20 seconds, in
microseconds. The loop has the bodies of the fill as well (they are built on
that thread with \fBingest\-threads 1\fR) and the commit at the end of a batch
.TP 8
\fBstore.fill.images\fR, \fB.image_bytes\fR, \fB.validate_ns\fR, \fB.validate_ns_per_image\fR
0.7: zone images the Backend took from the loader since the start (a first fill,
a reload, a fetch), their size in bytes, the time spent in
\fBzone_image_validate_ex()\fR (the check that every image passes before it is
stored or served) in nanoseconds in all and per image
.TP 8
\fBstore.fill.invalid\fR, \fB.store_only\fR, \fB.resident\fR
Images refused by that check (the zone is rejected, \fBkarictl dbstatus
<zone>\fR says why); images of a zone that was not in memory, which went to the
store as they are (a background fill: no entry, no registry change; the zone is
attached from its record when the first query comes); images installed as the
zone's version in memory
.TP 8
\fBxfr.view\fR, \fBxfr.records\fR, \fBxfr.image_bytes\fR, \fBxfr.puts\fR, \fBxfr.puts_uptodate\fR, \fBxfr.put_nospc\fR, \fBxfr.put_errors\fR, \fBxfr.state_writes\fR, \fBxfr.state_stale\fR, \fBxfr.state_skipped\fR, \fBxfr.orphans_deleted\fR, \fBxfr.unstorable\fR, \fBxfr.restored\fR, \fBxfr.restored_expired\fR, \fBxfr.file_won\fR, \fBxfr.restore_bad\fR, \fBxfr.restore_identity\fR, \fBxfr.restore_pending\fR, \fBxfr.restore_ms\fR, \fBxfr.restore_timeouts\fR, \fBxfr.gc_deleted\fR, \fBxfr.stale_capped\fR
0.7, the \fBsecondaries:\fR part of the text form: the copies of the secondary
zones in the store's view \fB_karidns\-xfr\fR (none: \fBxfr.view = none\fR) and
what the writes after a refresh, the restore at the start and the GC did since
the start; karidns(8) "Secondary zones: the copies in the zone store"
.TP 8
\fBxfr.rx_texts\fR, \fBxfr.rx_bytes\fR, \fBxfr.rx_capped\fR, \fBxfr.rx_restored\fR, \fBxfr.rx_lost\fR
0.7: the settings that primaries sent (Extended AXFR version 3) kept in
\fBsettings.dat\fR: the texts there and the octets of their records (at most
131,072 texts and 16 MiB), the new texts not kept because that cap was reached,
the restores that put a zone's received settings in force again, and the
restores that found them not kept (the zone asks for the whole zone at its first
refresh)
.TP 8
\fBsecondary.expired_zones\fR, \fB.expired_stale\fR, \fB.expired_servfail\fR, \fB.stale_answers\fR, \fB.expire_events\fR
0.7: the secondaries past their expire deadline (the control thread's last walk
of the secondaries), of them those answered from stale data (serve-stale, EDE 3)
and those answered SERVFAIL (EDE 24); the answers given from stale data, and the
\fBexpired\fR events since the start
.TP 8
\fBstore.recon.guard.held_views\fR
Views whose sweep the mass-delete guard holds back (0 normally). With 1 or more:
\fB.view\fR, \fB.unlisted\fR (zones the pass did not list), \fB.total\fR,
\fB.since_unix\fR and \fB.release\fR (\fBkarictl reload \-force\fR)
.TP 8
\fBstore.zone.name\fR, \fB.view\fR, \fB.zid\fR, \fB.state\fR
With \fBzone <name>\fR: the record of one zone (only these keys, besides
\fBschema\fR and \fBstore.time.*\fR): name, view, zone id, \fBstored\fR or
\fBknown\fR
.TP 8
\fBstore.zone.serial\fR, \fB.flags\fR, \fB.source\fR, \fB.source_id\fR, \fB.stamp_gen\fR, \fB.stamp_time\fR, \fB.settings\fR
SOA serial of the stored version, flag bits, source number and the zone's id in
that database, version stamp, settings id
.TP 8
\fBstore.zone.image_bytes\fR, \fB.segment\fR, \fB.offset\fR, \fB.digest\fR
Size of the image, where it lies (segment and byte offset; not for a known
zone), digest of the zone content (hex)
.TP 8
\fBstore.zone.attached\fR
1 when the zone is attached now (where the server can tell)
.TP 8
\fBstore.zone.elem\fR, \fB.resident\fR, \fB.staged\fR, \fB.elem_state\fR
0.7, for a zone that does not answer: the attach table has an element for the
zone, the element has its data, a worker's attach of it is in the staging table
and not registered yet, the element's state (\fBresident\fR, \fBloading\fR,
\fBerror\fR, \fBabsent\fR; \fBnone\fR without an element). Read only
.TP 8
\fBstore.zone.att_claim\fR, \fB.att_claim_req\fR, \fB.att_claim_workers\fR, \fB.fetch_claim\fR
0.7: a claim for the attach of the zone is held (the workers that waited for a
store thread joined it), its request is in a ring, the workers that wait (a bit
set: bit n is worker n, bit 15 every worker from 15 up), and the claim for the
fetch of the zone from the database is held. A claim that stays held while
nothing is attached or fetched is a request that was lost
.PP
A bad argument, a zone that is not in the store (\fBERROR zone not found\fR), a
name in several views without a view, or \fBzone\fR without a store are answered
\fBERROR ...\fR (exit status 3).
.RE
.TP 8
\fBstore compact\fR, \fBstore checkpoint\fR, \fBstore scrub start\fR, \fBstore scrub stop\fR
Change the store (0.7); they are not read commands, so they need the main
control key (a restricted key, read-only or with \fBallow\-commands\fR, does not
have them). \fBstore compact\fR asks the cleaner to run without its rate limit
until no segment is dead enough (it returns at once; \fBstore.compact.*\fR shows
the progress), then the server writes a checkpoint and removes the segments that
are empty. The cleaner runs by itself, at 16 MiB a second at most, whenever a
segment is half dead; this is for after a mass change, or before a
measurement. \fBstore checkpoint\fR writes a checkpoint now (the journal is cut,
what was written is durable) and answers when it is done. \fBstore scrub
start\fR begins a scrub pass at once (the scrub reads every record, at most 16
MiB a second; \fBstore\-scrub\-interval\fR starts one by itself); \fBstore scrub
stop\fR ends the pass that runs. The answers are \fBOK ...\fR or \fBERROR ...\fR
(exit status 3).
.RS 8
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karictl store compact
karictl store \-k | grep '^store.compact.'
karictl store scrub start
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP 8
\fBtrace [level]\fR
Change the debug level in force of the server (0.7; \fBrndc trace\fR): without
an argument one more (at most 99), with one that level, a number from 0 to
99. The reply is \fBOK debug level is now N\fR; a level that is not a number
from 0 to 99 is answered \fBERROR trace: the debug level must be a number from 0
to 99\fR, a second argument \fBERROR trace takes one argument at most: ...\fR
(exit status 3, the level is not changed). The server logs each change as
\fB[Control] debug level is now N\fR (category \fBgeneral\fR, notice). The
channels with \fBseverity dynamic\fR write the messages up to the level, and the
built-in \fBdefault_debug\fR (the file \fBkaridns.run\fR in \fBoptions {
directory }\fR) writes everything up to it while it is above 0; see karidns(8),
\fBlogging\fR. The level is not kept across a restart (\fBkaridns \-d\fR sets it
at the start). Every process of the server follows (0.7): the sandboxed process,
which has the control channel, hands the level to the supervisor (with the
supervisors of the plugins and of \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR), the UDP routers, the
connect broker and the loader, and a loader that is started again later comes up
with the level in force.
.RS 8
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karictl trace 3      # the debug 3 lines of query\-errors (why a query was REFUSED) appear in the dynamic channels
karictl notrace
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP 8
\fBnotrace\fR
Set the debug level to 0 (\fBrndc notrace\fR); the reply is \fBOK debug level is
now 0\fR. An argument is refused (\fBERROR notrace takes no argument\fR).
.RS 8
.PP
\fBtrace\fR and \fBnotrace\fR need the permission \fBtrace\fR of a restricted
control key (\fBallow\-commands { trace; }\fR); a \fBread\-only yes\fR key alone
does not have it.
.RE
.TP 8
\fBtsig\-keygen [keyname]\fR
Generate a 256-bit random TSIG secret with OpenSSL \fBRAND_bytes(3)\fR and print
a \fBkey\fR block (\fBalgorithm hmac\-sha256\fR) ready for
\fBkaridns.conf\fR. The default key name is \fBtransfer\-key\fR. This command
does not contact the server and does not read the configuration file.
.RS 8
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karictl tsig\-keygen transfer\-key
.fi
.RE
.RE
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE (karictl.conf)
By default, \fBkarictl\fR reads the shared secret from
\fB/usr/local/etc/karidns/karictl.conf\fR. The package installs
\fBkarictl.conf.sample\fR next to it.
.SS Configuration Syntax
.RS 2
.nf
socket "/var/run/karidns/control.sock";
\&
key "karictl" {
    algorithm "hmac\-sha256";
    secret "UkVQTEFDRS1NRTpvcGVuc3NsLXJhbmQtYmFzZTY0LTMy"; // placeholder: replace with `openssl rand \-base64 32`
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
The \fBsecret\fR defined in \fBkarictl.conf\fR must match the secret configured
in the \fBcontrol\-channel\fR block of \fBkaridns.conf\fR.
.PP
The file is not parsed as a full configuration file: \fBkarictl\fR takes the
quoted value that follows the \fBfirst\fR occurrence of the word \fBsecret\fR,
and the quoted value that follows the first occurrence of the word \fBsocket\fR,
anywhere in the file \fBoutside comments (\fB#\fB, \fB//\fB, \fB/* */\fB) and
quoted strings\fR (the sample's own comment mentions \fB{ secret "..."; }\fR,
which an earlier version took for the secret). White space after the \fB=\fR
padding of the secret is ignored. The value after the first \fBalgorithm\fR
(quoted or not) selects the HMAC: \fBhmac\-md5\fR, \fBhmac\-sha1\fR,
\fBhmac\-sha224\fR, \fBhmac\-sha256\fR (default), \fBhmac\-sha384\fR or
\fBhmac\-sha512\fR; it must match the server's \fBcontrol\-channel { algorithm
}\fR, and an unsupported name exits with status 2. The key name is not used. The
secret may decode to at most 256 bytes. A file that holds a \fBsecret\fR and
that group or other users may read or write is refused (exit status 2, with the
\fBchmod 600\fR that fixes it), as the server refuses such a
\fBconninfo\-file\fR: whoever reads the secret can run every command the key
allows. This applies to any file given with \fB\-c\fR that holds the secret,
\fBkaridns.conf\fR included. The file is opened once and its mode checked on
that descriptor. Each command word must be non-empty and free of white space and
control characters, the command line at most 510 bytes, and a command takes at
most three arguments (as \fBstats\fR and \fBstore\fR already checked): otherwise
exit status 2 before anything is sent.
.PP
\fBRestricted keys.\fR The server can hold several keys, each allowed to run
only some commands (\fBcontrol\-channel { keys { } }\fR in \fBkaridns.conf\fR,
"Restricted control keys"). \fBkarictl\fR needs nothing for that: give each user
a \fBkarictl.conf\fR with the \fBsecret\fR and the \fBalgorithm\fR of the key it
should use. The server finds the key by the response to its challenge. A command
the key may not run is answered with \fBERROR command not allowed for key
"<name>"\fR and \fBkarictl\fR exits with status 3; the server logs it with the
key name. To let a user other than the server's in, the server's
\fBcontrol\-channel { allow\-group }\fR must name a group that user belongs to
(the socket is then group-writable); the user needs the \fBkarictl.conf\fR of
its key and no access to \fBkaridns.conf\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 8
\fB0\fR
Command executed successfully (including \fBtsig\-keygen\fR and \fB\-v\fR).
.TP 8
\fB1\fR
Socket path too long, connection failure to the control socket, or failure to
receive the challenge.
.TP 8
\fB2\fR
Usage error (no command, an unknown option, \fBnotify\fR / \fBretransfer\fR /
\fBzonestatus\fR / \fBrollback\fR without a zone name; the usage text goes to
standard error and nothing is sent to the server), or the secret could not be
read or decoded from the configuration file, the challenge was invalid, or
authentication failed. A command the server does not know is not a usage error
for \fBkarictl\fR: the server answers \fBERROR unknown command\fR and the exit
status is 3. \fB\-h\fR and \fB\-\-help\fR print the usage text to standard
output and exit with 0.
.TP 8
\fB3\fR
The server returned an error (\fBERROR ...\fR, including a failed \fBreload\fR
or \fBreconfig\fR, a \fBtrace\fR level that is not a number from 0 to 99, a
command that the key is not allowed to run, a database zone that was rejected or
could not be read, and a reload that timed out) or an invalid \fBstatus\fR or
\fBobservatory\fR response (also \fBdbstatus\fR without a database block).
.SH FILES
.TP 8
\fB/usr/local/etc/karidns/karictl.conf\fR
Default control channel client configuration file.
.TP 8
\fB/var/run/karidns/control.sock\fR
Default UNIX domain socket endpoint monitored by the \fBkaridns\fR daemon.
